window.PROJECTS = [{"name": "Werbos", "tagline": "Smaller, faster, owned AI that grades itself outside the model", "oneLiner": "Werbos is a local-first AI assistant built from scratch since 2026-05-01: its own agent loop, router, reasoning engine, retrieval library and model stack, held to a rule that no model ever grades a model.", "problem": "Frontier models are rented, slow, costly to run around the clock, and confidently wrong. Small local models are cheap and private but bluff more. The usual fix, an LLM judge, fails quietly: a model grading a model rubber-stamps its own mistakes. Werbos tests a different bet. Move the knowing into a curated library, route and compute outside the model, and grade every answer against an oracle the system cannot bribe, so a small owned model can be fast, cheap, and honest about what it does not know.", "whatItIs": "A working alternative to big-LLM assistants, shipped as a web app, Mac app and CLI at werbos.netlify.app. One chat surface fronts a deterministic stack: an intent compiler, a 295-skill router, bounded solvers, a 3,700-folder retrieval library and an owned numpy model, with a small model used only as the voice. The core discipline is three rules. Every grader answers to an oracle it cannot bribe. A thin oracle abstains rather than bluffs. A verdict may only act (prune, promote, run unattended) after it has been earned. The system is documented as 169 components and 13 self-improvement loops on its public flow page, and every day of the build is logged from Day 0 (2026-05-01).", "howItWorks": ["Intent first: parsetongue strips format codepoints and compiles input to a typed Intent through three paths (slash command, path-plus-verb, LLM classify). The fast paths return in sub-millisecond; the LLM path (10 to 30 s on a local 8B) is the fallback.", "Deterministic skill router: IDF-weighted term scoring picks among 295 skills outside the model and fires only above a score and margin threshold, else falls through to chat. It exists because a 3B drops operators and leaks nil tool calls when asked to emit them.", "Precook cache and complexity router: repeat asks serve from a SQLite cache in under 50 ms; misses are classified SIMPLE, MEDIUM or COMPLEX and sent to the smallest model tier that fits.", "reason_tree: a goal is decomposed toward base cases and composed back up, every node Merkle-hashed, so a repeated subgoal is proven once and an unprovable one is an honest GAP instead of a guess.", "Bounded deterministic lanes run first: puzzle_solver enumerates every arrangement of a constraint puzzle and answers the unique solution, or reports underdetermined or contradictory (it caught two broken ChatGPT-built puzzles in about 4 ms); reason_qa composes card facts with a proof; grounding_guard ships a reworded answer only if its numbers and names survive.", "Retrieval as memory: a conversation is its own small card library, so a fact that scrolled out of the context window comes back when it matters (\"my CEO is Sarah\" resurfaces as \"Sarah is your CEO\") at constant memory cost.", "Knowledge lane and brick bin: general facts route through a 98-source capability index to a grounded, cited answer or an honest abstain; code requests compose from 370 execution-verified bricks, run, and return the result or name the missing brick.", "Owned model stack (werbos/weld): byte-BPE tokenizer, numpy transformer runtime, reverse-mode autograd checked against finite differences, trainer, and a signed content-addressed frozen brain. Open-weight models serve only as teachers and baselines.", "Deploy: Docker Compose plus systemd on a homelab server, behind nginx with watchtower, warden and beacon runtimes; Hugo site on Netlify with a client-side demo that runs with no server."], "aiAngle": ["Evals before features: frozen, content-hashed probe sets with gold computed in Python, so no model is in the verdict. A green-to-red change under the same bundle hash is a caught regression, not drift. Ten of sixty reasoning probes are unanswerable on purpose, to measure whether the system abstains.", "Un-bribable grading: an auditor classifies every grader on a leakage spectrum and refuses a verb a verdict it cannot earn (Day 49, it caught two). A known-answer bank of human-authored (question, true answer) pairs is checked before any model-written test, because the soft judge had marked misroutes as ok.", "HAIRLy: Claude plays a real user through the actual chat UI in six typing personas, and an AI grade and a free deterministic grade must agree or the turn goes to a human. Its first live turn (a Nobel Prize question) caught a routing bug that robotic test queries never hit.", "werbos probe, \"rspec for LLMs\": one describe/it/expect spec runs unchanged against werbos, Claude or raw open weights, with real tool mocks, allowed failures and a memory-growth sampler. Pointed at everything (571 tests) on Day 140, it found bugs in the product, the server and the test framework itself.", "Conversation judge and structure ratchet: Claude reads a 15-turn chat as a person would (4/10 to a first PASS at 7/10, once facts were authored warm at the source). The ratchet replays 390 gold turns over 80 chats hourly, grades answer, readability and fit, and holds out 20 conversations whose replies are never read.", "Measured results: against a bare 3B, cards doubled solved problems (8/50 to 16/50), raised trap refusals from 3/10 to 10/10 and cut confident wrong answers from 48 to 13. On the 500-row honest-abstain gauntlet wrong answers fell to 4 (99% honest); an adversarial cascade took sealed-gauntlet honesty from 12% to 100% with capability unchanged.", "Execution-graded coding: 478/478 coding probes versus 471 for qwen2.5:14b, and 26/26 DOM-graded codeask versus 21/26 under a fair grader, at 0.2 ms versus about 5 s median.", "Honest negative results are logged: a 1M-param model beat every from-scratch reader from 3.4M to 57M on a full GPU day; the owned reader stayed out of the chat until it cleared honesty; two features were killed by the harness the day they were built; a probe run lost 20 rows to that day's own changes."], "stack": ["Python", "NumPy", "Ollama", "MLX", "Starlette", "Docker", "systemd", "Bun", "Hugo", "Netlify", "React", "Tailwind"], "metrics": [{"label": "Commits (main repo)", "value": "2,113"}, {"label": "Flow components documented", "value": "169"}, {"label": "Skills behind the router", "value": "295"}, {"label": "Self-improvement loops", "value": "13"}, {"label": "Test functions", "value": "8,502"}, {"label": "Library cards (3,687 topics)", "value": "1.26M"}, {"label": "Days of daily log", "value": "160 (Day 0 to 159)"}, {"label": "Honest rate, 500-row gauntlet", "value": "99% (4 wrong)"}], "status": "In active development, live site and demo", "timeline": "Apr 2026 – Nov 2026 (site since Dec 2025)", "progress": ["Live now: a public site, a client-side demo and a Mac app, with 2,113 commits across Day 0 to Day 159 and a structure ratchet running every hour.", "The owned model ladder is complete, and a 336M chat model was promoted on Day 104 as the first checkpoint where scale did not hurt. A 350M pretrain over about 4.8B tokens was started on rented GPU.", "Honesty is the strongest number: 4 wrong out of 500 on the gauntlet, with the remaining misses tracked as a work list.", "Known gap, from the project's own changelog: the Gatekeep token verifier exists but is not wired into the server, so the security posture is still loopback-only. That, not model size, is what stands between here and a client deployment.", "Next: the first external user who would miss it if it vanished."], "dogfooding": ["Runs 24/7 on Jason's homelab server under systemd timers: skill-smoke every 30 minutes, the train daemon every 2 hours; the dashboard's custom-model toggle answers from owned weights on CPU.", "His own chats are archived as .pfm files, and the dogfood-replay loop mines them for moments he was testing it and feeds those back into the eval suites.", "He operates it through the dashboard (home, chat, agents, scheduler, integrations, an Autopilot panel) and a Slack bridge; Telegram and WhatsApp bridges share the same skills and personas.", "The phone persona answers inbound scam calls as Walter, a friendly 78-year-old retiree, over Twilio with local speech and an indexed transcript of every call.", "Other products consume it: CryptoBlocks' VibeBlocks posts plain-English requests to werbos's /compose endpoint and gets back a brick tree built from verified bricks; Werbos.design is a sibling site generator that composes from a brief with no LLM at all.", "Dependency fixes in its own repo come from Jason's Mycelium scanner (merged PRs #9 to #11), and werbos carries a verifier for tokens from his Gatekeep SSO. The site's FAQ bot is built from the same grounded-answer shell.", "Trained on his own corpus: the first promoted model (werbos-0.0.1, Day 30) scored 1.51x baseline, and every day since is logged, including the days the claims were wrong."], "links": [{"label": "Live site", "url": "https://werbos.netlify.app/"}, {"label": "Benchmarks", "url": "https://werbos.netlify.app/benchmarks/"}, {"label": "Live demo", "url": "https://werbos.netlify.app/demo/"}], "buildLogTitle": "reasoning gauntlet, bare 3B vs cards (benchmarks/v2, Aug 2026)", "buildLog": "$ python scripts/reason_gauntlet.py --reader ollama:llama3.2:3b\n$ python scripts/shape_instantiate.py --reader ollama:llama3.2:3b\n\n                        SOLVED (50)   REFUSED (10)   lies   instantiated\nbare llama3.2:3b          8  (16%)      3  (30%)      48        -\ncards + llama3.2:3b      16  (32%)     10 (100%)      13      29/51\nbare owned 336m           0  ( 0%)      7  (70%)      32        -\ncards + owned 336m        1  ( 2%)     10 (100%)       1       2/51\nbare owned 1M             1  ( 2%)     10 (100%)       1        -\ncards + owned 1M          0  ( 0%)     10 (100%)       0       0/51\n\n                  3B v1  3B v2   owned 336m\nCHECK RECALL       44%    59%       97%      of wrong answers, rejected\nCHECK COST          5%     6%        0%      of RIGHT answers, wrongly killed\n\nv1   set-count-overlap    0 pass   0 abstain   5 wrong\nv2   set-count-overlap    0 pass   5 abstain   0 wrong\n\n# describe-it (12) / codeask (26), execution-graded\nwerbos owned composer   12/12   26/26   median 34 ms / 0.2 ms\nqwen2.5:14b (fair)      12/12   21/26   median 12,390 ms / 5,013 ms\n\n# honest-abstain gauntlet, 500 rows (Sep 2026)\nDay 141   355 right   131 abstain   14 wrong   97% honest\nDay 142   362 right   133 abstain    4 wrong   99% honest\nfull probe (571)   292 -> 403 (D141) -> 414 (D142)\n# coding gauntlet, execution-graded (Aug 2026)\nwerbos bricks 478/478  vs  qwen2.5:14b 471   (~89x faster, 20 bricks serve 478 problems)", "primitives": [{"name": "pitts", "role": "Agent loop: think, act, observe, repeat, with tool dispatch.", "proof": "Spec v1, no runtime deps; 247 import lines inside werbos."}, {"name": "mcculloch", "role": "Eval primitive: Suites of Probes graded into Reports, never generating text.", "proof": "MCCULLOCH-EVAL-v1; the pair to pitts, which runs models while mcculloch judges them."}, {"name": "pfm", "role": ".pfm container for agent output: content, chain, tools and checksum in one file.", "proof": "224 tests, published to npm as get-pfm; werbos archives its chats as .pfm."}, {"name": "partyparrot (+ partyparrot-py)", "role": "Real-time presence and soft-lock wire protocol, Bun reference server, Python client.", "proof": "46 tests / 351 assertions; werbos uses the Python client for networked tic-tac-toe sessions archived to PFM."}, {"name": "observe", "role": "The see layer: trigger sources, a restricted predicate language, HMAC webhook auth.", "proof": "v1.0.0 locked 2026-05-08 with 98 conformance tests; 24 import lines in werbos."}, {"name": "watchtower", "role": "Protocol for background work that yields to humans: triggers, dispatches, approvals, audit log.", "proof": "v1.0.0, 89 tests, default-deny shell and fn dispatch; runs in the deploy stack with a React console."}, {"name": "warden", "role": "The defend layer: rate, pattern, predicate and allowlist rules that raise alerts.", "proof": "v1.0.0; alerts feed watchtower's audit chain; HTTP runtime in the deploy stack."}, {"name": "beacon", "role": "The signal layer: health probes (http, tcp, process, file, disk, git ref) and state transitions.", "proof": "v1.1.0, six probe types, BEACON-RUNTIME-v1 service beside watchtower."}, {"name": "glasshouse", "role": "Trace inspector: one trace_id ties parse, model, tool and reply into a timeline.", "proof": "Backs the debug walk and autopilot watch; every turn writes a trace."}, {"name": "soma, capture, learn", "role": "Index of loop primitives: grade, trigger, fence, an append-only experience log, a champion/challenger gate.", "proof": "werbos's ExperienceStore adopted soma.capture so every loop logs one shape."}, {"name": "Merkle Mini Model (mmm-poc, mmm-brain)", "role": "Forest of tiny single-domain models behind a deterministic conductor that routes, composes or abstains.", "proof": "4M-param from-scratch leaves; the library-scale tests use a model-free four-valued verdict."}, {"name": "Gatekeep", "role": "Jason's passkey (WebAuthn) single sign-on server issuing Ed25519 OIDC tokens.", "proof": "78 tests / 224 assertions; werbos carries a stdlib-only verifier, gatekeep_auth.py (11 tests, pinned to Gatekeep's vectors), not yet wired into the server."}, {"name": "Mycelium", "role": "Cross-repo vulnerability scanner that opens fix pull requests.", "proof": "Opened werbos PRs #9 to #11, each upgrading 15 vulnerable packages, all merged."}], "loops": [{"name": "GR.1 games ratchet", "what": "A deterministic game engine (Nim) generates and grades reasoning probes with no model in the grade; the ratchet ran 26/26."}, {"name": "HL.1 HAIRLy", "what": "Claude acts as a user in the real chat UI across six personas; AI and deterministic grades merge, and disagreements go to a human."}, {"name": "HL.2 werbos probe", "what": "One spec runs against werbos, Claude and open weights, with real mocks, a persona-fuzz layer and a run history timeline."}, {"name": "HL.3 conversation judge", "what": "Claude reads a scripted 15-turn chat as a person and scores how human it feels, never seeing the right answer."}, {"name": "HL.4 structure ratchet", "what": "Replays 390 gold turns hourly, grades reply shape on a ladder, holds out 20 unread conversations, and fails a commit that drops a rung."}, {"name": "SE.1.1 skill-smoke", "what": "A systemd timer runs every prompt skill about every 30 minutes, grades format markers and writes a trend and a training-fuel row."}, {"name": "SE.3 curriculum", "what": "Picks coding problems from a bank, has the local model solve them, judges them, and adapts difficulty to streaks."}, {"name": "SE.7.x corpus, eval, train daemon", "what": "Builds a deduped training set from confirmed passes, trains every 2 hours, and promotes only if the composite eval clears a floor."}, {"name": "SE.2 autofix", "what": "Reads the skill-smoke trend, diagnoses regressions (marker too strict, model floor, variance) and writes reviewable fix candidates, read-only."}, {"name": "SE.5.1 cache warmer", "what": "Pre-renders precookable skills overnight so the first live ask is served from cache."}, {"name": "A.4 dogfood-replay", "what": "Scans archived .pfm chats for moments where Jason was clearly testing, and replays them against the current model."}, {"name": "Autopilot", "what": "Predicts a skill and its expected result, acts on an allowlist, judges, corrects, and promotes a learned routing policy only if it beats the champion on held-out data."}, {"name": "Training-fuel factory", "what": "One 374-domain map drives card, corpus and conversation minting on a burst-rented GPU, graded and deduped before anything is kept."}], "milestones": [{"day": "Day 0 (2026-05-01)", "text": "From brew install ollama to a fine-tuned 3B that knows his favorite color."}, {"day": "Day 7 (2026-05-08)", "text": "38 commits and tests 1,266 to 1,903 in one day; the first sprint went from 0% to 100%."}, {"day": "Day 30 (2026-06-02)", "text": "werbos-0.0.1 promoted: composite 0.1201 against a 0.0795 baseline, 1.51x."}, {"day": "Day 43 (2026-06-13)", "text": "First frozen, hashed benchmark: a 3B plus a library beat the raw 3B 3x and tied Claude on factual recall."}, {"day": "Day 48 (2026-06-18)", "text": "Every judgement moved outside the model, including a known-answer grader."}, {"day": "Day 63 (2026-07-14)", "text": "Owned model ladder finished end to end: tokenizer, runtime, autograd, trainer, signed frozen brain."}, {"day": "Day 64 (2026-07-15)", "text": "Adversarial cascade took honesty from 12% to 100%; the daemon rolled back a regression on its third cycle."}, {"day": "Day 85 (2026-08-04)", "text": "Beat a 14B model at coding, 478 execution-graded probes to 471, never once wrong."}, {"day": "Day 107 (2026-08-27)", "text": "Cards took a 3B from 8/50 to 16/50 and cut its lies 48 to 13; on the owned 336M they cut lies 32 to 1."}, {"day": "Day 138 (2026-09-25)", "text": "HAIRLy shipped: Claude becomes the user, and its first turn caught a real routing bug."}, {"day": "Day 142 (2026-09-29)", "text": "500-row gauntlet at 362 right, 133 honest abstains, 4 wrong: 99% honest."}, {"day": "Days 148 to 159 (2026-10-15)", "text": "246 commits built the structure ratchet, which kept improving on conversations werbos never saw."}], "slug": "werbos", "n": 2, "platforms": "Web · Mac", "category": "Applied AI", "shots": [{"src": "img/werbos-00.jpg", "caption": "The live Werbos dashboard on Jason's homelab: what it knows (89k across 152 libraries), routing fit, autopilot (704 actions), and the 478/478 gauntlet vs qwen2.5:14b", "w": 1400, "h": 875}, {"src": "img/werbos-01.jpg", "caption": "Werbos chat: a 17 ms social answer from the owned brain, and an honest \"I won't guess\" when nothing grounds the answer", "w": 1400, "h": 875}, {"src": "img/werbos-02.jpg", "caption": "Skills: each one runs from the CLI (werbos <name>) and from chat (/<name>), with per-skill config and a Run preview", "w": 1400, "h": 875}, {"src": "img/werbos-03.jpg", "caption": "Mind: Werbos's inner life, with monologue, portrait, honesty, dreams and calibration tabs", "w": 1400, "h": 875}, {"src": "img/werbos-04.jpg", "caption": "Memory: what Werbos remembers about its operator between conversations", "w": 1400, "h": 875}, {"src": "img/werbos-05.jpg", "caption": "Models: 15 checkpoints Werbos trained itself, no open weights", "w": 1400, "h": 875}, {"src": "img/werbos-06.jpg", "caption": "Autopilot: Werbos runs its own skills (predict, act, observe, correct), then learns", "w": 1400, "h": 875}, {"src": "img/werbos-07.jpg", "caption": "Agents: each is a persona, a role and a permissions surface", "w": 1400, "h": 875}, {"src": "img/werbos-08.jpg", "caption": "MCP both ways: Claude Desktop, Claude Code, Cursor and Zed can use Werbos, and outside bots plug in", "w": 1400, "h": 875}, {"src": "img/werbos-09.jpg", "caption": "Knowledge hub: every owned card, readable. Nothing here is generated.", "w": 1400, "h": 875}, {"src": "img/werbos-10.jpg", "caption": "werbos.netlify.app home page", "w": 1400, "h": 875}, {"src": "img/werbos-11.jpg", "caption": "Browser demo: pick topic packs, mint a model, talk to it", "w": 1400, "h": 875}, {"src": "img/werbos-12.jpg", "caption": "Benchmarks page: frozen, hashed eval sets", "w": 1400, "h": 875}, {"src": "img/werbos-13.jpg", "caption": "The Flow page: all 169 components, layer by layer", "w": 1400, "h": 972}, {"src": "img/werbos-14.jpg", "caption": "The library graph: 1,258,827 cards across 3,687 topics", "w": 1400, "h": 875}]}, {"name": "Mycelium", "tagline": "One map of every dependency risk across all your repos", "oneLiner": "Mycelium maps vulnerable dependencies across dozens of projects at once, ranks them by real exposure, and opens tested fix pull requests, with an AI reviewer that tries to break each change.", "problem": "Dependabot-style tools work one repository at a time, which falls apart with dozens of projects. Teams cannot easily answer whether a new advisory affects them, where, how much it matters, and who will fix it.", "whatItIs": "A TypeScript tool with a CLI, a web dashboard, and an MCP server. It reads every project's packages (direct and transitive) and infrastructure, checks them against OSV.dev and deps.dev, ranks findings by severity, exposure and how many projects share the issue, then applies upgrades in a throwaway branch, verifies, and opens a PR. It never merges.", "howItWorks": ["Map: parsers for npm, Go, Python, Gradle and more build a package graph including transitive dependencies, plus containers, CI, hosting and domain checks.", "Watch: batches package versions to OSV.dev and deps.dev, stores results in SQLite, and syncs on a systemd timer.", "Rank: scores by severity, per-project exposure (public site vs laptop tool), and blast radius across projects.", "Fix: applies the upgrade in a separate git worktree, rescans to confirm the vulnerable version is gone, runs the project's build and tests, then opens a PR. A fix-all mode does this headless across every project.", "MCP server exposes nine tools (scan, check, projects, findings, impact, why, fix_plan, fix_apply, audit) so AI agents can drive it.", "Deploy: runs as a service on Jason's home server behind sign-in with his own Gatekeep auth, reachable over a private tailnet."], "aiAngle": ["Adversarial PR reviewer built on Claude: it tries to break the change and either returns notes or marks it ready for a human.", "Least-privilege agent: the reviewer can only read through a small tool set, cannot run code or write, and treats all PR content as data, not instructions.", "Fails closed: a review that errors, times out, or is stopped by a safety classifier ends as an error, never a green check.", "Output is sanitized so model text cannot ping users, load images, add links, or forge the pass marker.", "Mixed lenses: AI agents (adversarial, Crimson exploitability, accessibility) run beside deterministic ones (secret key watcher, vulnerable-package check, deprecated-code janitor) on separate polls so a stalled model call cannot block the rules.", "Privacy by default: only package names and versions leave the machine; AI reviews send PR contents to Anthropic with likely-secret files excluded."], "stack": ["TypeScript", "Node.js", "SQLite", "OSV.dev", "deps.dev", "Claude API", "MCP", "GitHub API", "systemd"], "metrics": [{"label": "Projects watched (live)", "value": "83"}, {"label": "Clean projects (live)", "value": "61"}, {"label": "Upgrades shipped", "value": "669"}, {"label": "Fix PRs merged or open", "value": "81 PRs, 45 projects"}, {"label": "AI PR reviews", "value": "26"}, {"label": "Tests passing", "value": "149"}, {"label": "MCP tools", "value": "9"}], "status": "Shipping, running in production on own server", "timeline": "Nov 2026 (64 commits, built in about 5 days)", "progress": ["Live dashboard, Nov 11 2026: 83 projects watched, 61 clean, 669 package upgrades shipped through 81 fix PRs across 45 projects (666 merged, 5 awaiting merge).", "Scanner, graph, ranking and dashboard shipped.", "One-click and headless fix-all with verified rescans and PRs.", "MCP server and a fix-all-deps Claude skill.", "Review agents added: adversarial reviewer, Crimson, accessibility, key watcher, fungi, janitor.", "Reviewer hardened to fail closed. Known gaps: no reachability analysis yet, GitHub-only PRs."], "dogfooding": ["Runs on Jason's homelab server behind his own Gatekeep sign-in, watching all 83 of his projects; the AI reviewer has run 26 PR reviews on his Claude plan (8 sent back, 18 ready to merge).", "Runs on Jason's own server watching 82 of his repositories.", "README reports open vulnerabilities cut from about 700 to about 40 in a few days.", "Its fix commits are in 26 of Jason's project repos (40 'mycelium: upgrade N vulnerable packages' commits), including Werbos.", "Saved fix-all run reports show repeated headless runs across his projects."], "links": [{"label": "GitHub", "url": "https://github.com/jasonsutter87/mycelium"}], "buildLogTitle": "test run and recent commits, Nov 2026", "buildLog": "$ npm test\nok 1 - known key formats are caught, placeholders and marked test values aren't\nok 17 - a repo this account can't post to records its result once and stops retrying\nok 41 - container checks\nok 65 - fix-all: waiting on upstream reads apart from needing a person, and groups by package\nok 81 - go.mod, requirements, gradle + catalog\nok 105 - the agent reads code with its tools, keeps thinking blocks in history, and returns a valid answer\nok 113 - what the model writes can't ping people, load images, add links, or fake the marker\nok 121 - a review that can't finish ends as an error, never a pass or a stuck pending check\nok 149 - a review stopped by a safety classifier fails closed, never a green check\n1..149\n# tests 149\n# pass 149\n# fail 0\n\n$ git log --oneline\n0f82cbe feat: Janitor agent flags expired @deprecate blocks on every PR (advisory)\n981473c fix: run the AI lenses on their own poll so they can't stall keys/fungi\n098d3d2 Crimson: reframe from 'hostile author' to 'naive author who may not know what the code enables'", "slug": "mycelium", "n": 3, "platforms": "Web · Server · MCP", "category": "Applied AI · Security", "shots": [{"src": "img/mycelium-00.jpg", "caption": "Projects: 83 watched, ranked by exposure; 61 clean", "w": 1400, "h": 620}, {"src": "img/mycelium-01.jpg", "caption": "Fixed: 671 fixes, 666 merged, every one a PR with the advisory linked", "w": 1400, "h": 620}, {"src": "img/mycelium-02.jpg", "caption": "Agents: an AI reviewer plus key watcher and fungi, each a GitHub check that never merges", "w": 1400, "h": 620}, {"src": "img/mycelium-03.jpg", "caption": "Crimson red-teams every PR for backdoors and exfiltration; an accessibility pass is available", "w": 1400, "h": 620}, {"src": "img/mycelium-04.jpg", "caption": "Findings ranked by blast radius across every project", "w": 1400, "h": 620}, {"src": "img/mycelium-05.jpg", "caption": "Infrastructure: CI, host and image warnings, such as unpinned GitHub Actions", "w": 1400, "h": 620}, {"src": "img/mycelium-06.jpg", "caption": "Email alerts for new critical and high findings", "w": 1400, "h": 620}, {"src": "img/mycelium-07.jpg", "caption": "Sign-in through Jason's own Gatekeep SSO", "w": 720, "h": 347}]}, {"name": "AutoDevDocs", "tagline": "Help docs written by using your running app", "oneLiner": "A black-box crawler that drives a live web app like a first-time user and produces its help pages, screenshots and a regression check, without ever reading the source code.", "problem": "Product docs go stale and describe features that no longer exist, and specs or test suites can be wrong too (one app's own tests selected on test ids that were never added). The only source that cannot lie about what an app does is the app running.", "whatItIs": "AutoDevDocs (repo: autodocumentor) points Playwright at a running app, detects its regions and controls from the rendered DOM and accessibility tree, clicks each control to record what it reveals, and captures screenshots and crops. The output is a structure map, a diff against the last crawl, a digest for writing, and a coverage report of controls no doc page mentions. A `check` command turns the crawl into a test that fails when the docs name a control that is gone. A Hugo brochure site sells it as a service.", "howItWorks": ["Project config is one JSON file per app: base URL, auth, a reset script, crawl targets, probes and flows, and which probe screenshot becomes which published image.", "detect.ts runs inside the page: it partitions the screen into top bar, ribbon, rails, panels and content by nearest landmark role first, then geometry when markup is non-semantic, and sees into open shadow roots.", "Probes reload the view, scroll a control into view, click it, and record the controls and headings that appear, plus a screenshot and a crop of the revealed panel. Delete and Sign out are always skipped, controls covered by an overlay are reported not clicked.", "Crawls record crashes, failed requests (network errors, HTTP 400+) and covered controls as problems; the previous run is kept so `diff` reports what appeared or went away and which doc pages mention it.", "`check` compares a crawl to an approved baseline and exits 1 on a regression. It also fails when a doc page names, in bold, a control that existed at approval and does not now. With usage data it ranks failures by the share of real sessions that use the control.", "Authentication is by a dedicated agent user or a saved browser session, never credentials in code; secrets live in gitignored env files."], "aiAngle": ["Black-box by design: the agent only sees the rendered DOM, accessibility tree and screenshots, so it can only document what a user can discover. This is a grounding rule enforced in the tool and in CLAUDE.md.", "Honest scope: the crawler and check are deterministic Playwright code, not an LLM loop. The authoring pass is done by a Claude session working from digest.md and screenshots, and the README roadmap lists turning that into an API call as not done yet.", "Docs become testable: 329 control names in Podium's help pages are verified against the live app on each check, so an LLM-written page that invents a button gets caught.", "Agent-friendly outputs: digest.md (everything seen), changes.md and coverage.md are structured so a model can revise only the pages affected by a change.", "Rule for the writing agent: when the crawl cannot say what a button does, check the running app rather than filling the gap from source."], "stack": ["TypeScript", "Playwright", "Node", "Hugo", "Tailwind", "Netlify", "Claude (authoring)"], "metrics": [{"label": "Commits", "value": "32 (autodocumentor) + 7 (site)"}, {"label": "Apps documented", "value": "5 live help sites"}, {"label": "Help pages generated", "value": "82 (showcase data)"}, {"label": "Doc terms verified (Podium)", "value": "329"}, {"label": "Crawl projects configured", "value": "11"}, {"label": "Source lines (src + scripts)", "value": "~1,700"}], "status": "Live site, in daily use on Jason's own apps", "timeline": "Jul 2026 first commit; main build Oct – Nov 2026", "progress": ["Jul 24, 2026: first black-box crawler. Oct 24 to 26: projects, revision reports, probes, flows, and the `check` regression mode with baselines.", "Oct 2026: Podium, Tallybook, WRITEpad, booktime, Glyphwork, Gatekeep, a TVS app and Quarry added as crawl projects.", "Nov 2026: Quarry's Outreach and Apply views and a browser-extension screenshot added.", "Brochure site live with pricing and a trial form. Roadmap: automate the authoring pass as an API call, probe deeper automatically, vision for unlabeled controls."], "dogfooding": ["Jason's own apps use it: published help centers for Podium, Tallybook, booktime, WRITEpad and Glyphwork all list autodocumentor as the source (site data shows 22, 15, 14, 19 and 12 pages).", "Repos say so: the booktime-site README says its help docs are written with autodocumentor, and the Glyphwork README says its screenshots come from it.", "Podium's check is run against the real help pages in Podium's site repo: it passed with 329 doc terms found in the live app and 48 flagged as deeper than the crawl.", "Jason states that it runs on push to main for most of his projects. I found no CI workflow in the repo, so treat that as his setup rather than something verified here."], "links": [{"label": "Product site", "url": "https://autodocumentor.netlify.app/"}, {"label": "Example generated help (Podium)", "url": "https://podium-elearning.netlify.app/docs/"}, {"label": "Tallybook help", "url": "https://tallybook-app.netlify.app/help/"}], "buildLogTitle": "npm run check -- podium, plus recent commits", "buildLog": "$ npm run check -- podium\n# Check: podium\nPassed, 1 warning(s).\n\n## Warnings\nNew since the baseline, or nothing to compare with. `approve` accepts them.\n- 48 control(s) the docs name are deeper than the crawl goes, so nothing\n  vouches for them yet; a flow would.\n\n$ git log --format='%h %ad %s' --date=short | head -8\ncd83b95 2026-11-04 Quarry: crawl the renamed Hide this search control\ne09b38f 2026-11-04 quarry: My profile label, taller nightly crop\n0bda6f6 2026-11-03 quarry: crawl Outreach and Apply, and a shot with the extension loaded\naeefbbd 2026-10-29 Add tvs project: sandbox stack, seed, crawl and form screenshots\n0d9269f 2026-10-28 glyphwork: crawl keyframe point editing for the help screenshots\n9146763 2026-10-28 Add glyphwork project: Studio crawl and help-center screenshots\ned89e78 2026-10-28 booktime: document the admin portal\needc39f 2026-10-27 booktime: demo-mode launcher and crawl config\nc8975a3 2026-10-26 check: the crawl as a test suite. Fails (exit 1) when a control or a panel\n                    a user had is gone, the page crashed, a request failed, a control is covered", "slug": "autodocumentor", "n": 4, "platforms": "Web · CI", "category": "Applied AI · Agents", "shots": [{"src": "img/autodocumentor-00.jpg", "caption": "autodocumentor.netlify.app: the product page with a real check run", "w": 1400, "h": 875}, {"src": "img/autodocumentor-01.jpg", "caption": "A help page it generated for Podium, with crawled screenshot", "w": 1400, "h": 875}, {"src": "img/autodocumentor-02.jpg", "caption": "A page from the Podium help center", "w": 1280, "h": 800}, {"src": "img/autodocumentor-03.jpg", "caption": "A page from the Tallybook help center", "w": 1280, "h": 800}, {"src": "img/autodocumentor-04.jpg", "caption": "A page from the booktime help center", "w": 1280, "h": 800}, {"src": "img/autodocumentor-05.jpg", "caption": "A page from the WRITEpad help center", "w": 1280, "h": 800}, {"src": "img/autodocumentor-06.jpg", "caption": "A page from the Glyphwork help center", "w": 1280, "h": 800}]}, {"name": "Podium", "tagline": "SCORM authoring: PowerPoint plus animation timeline plus quizzes", "oneLiner": "A course authoring tool for compliance training (food safety, harassment) that publishes to SCORM, xAPI and HTML and imports Articulate Storyline .story files.", "problem": "Training teams are locked into Articulate-style tools that are costly and hard to leave. They need accessible courses, voiceover and captions, and a way to bring existing Storyline content with them.", "whatItIs": "Podium is a Figma-style canvas editor for interactive eLearning: slides, themes, animations and effects, quizzes and score tracking, voiceovers and generated closed captions, with accessibility checking against WCAG. It runs in the browser and as a downloadable app for Mac, Windows and Linux, and exports to SCORM 1.2 and 2004, xAPI and standalone HTML. It was designed as a white-label product and succeeds an earlier TypeScript prototype called Lectern.", "howItWorks": ["Rust workspace of 22 crates plus 4 apps (server, studio, player-web, CLI); about 79,600 lines of Rust across 298 files", "Course is a JSON document validated by crates/core and a published JSON Schema; edits are invertible ops (crates/edit) giving undo/redo and full revision history in libSQL", "Player is a deterministic engine with no I/O: inputs in, effects out, so the same runtime serves the browser (WebAssembly), the studio preview and exported packages", "Storyline importer reads the .story zip (story.xml, slide XML, media), converts what has an equivalent, and returns a notes list of anything it could not carry over", "Narration uses native Kokoro text-to-speech via ONNX Runtime that returns audio plus per-sentence timings, which become WAV files and subtitles", "Accessibility crate checks a course against its target WCAG level: alt text, control names, captions, and text contrast with fallback to the container, fill or theme behind it", "Studio is Dioxus; the desktop build embeds the server in-process; multi-tenant auth (Argon2id), roles, Stripe billing with a 7-day trial"], "aiAngle": ["Slides are JSON with a published schema, and docs/ai-authoring.md is a guide written for a model that generates slides; validation and flow layout (rows, columns, grids instead of x/y) make model output editable by a person", "Theme-pack format is a tool-neutral public spec with a CLI check (`podium theme check`) so Claude or any model can author packs and the importer verifies them", "Local text-to-speech (Kokoro on ONNX Runtime) generates voiceover and captions with no cloud call", "Word and PowerPoint import crates (docx, pptx) turn existing documents into course drafts", "Built almost entirely with Claude Code multi-agent workflows: the repo carries review reports from senior-dev, archaeologist, refactor and API-architect agent passes, and a red-team and black-team pentest with a remediation log"], "stack": ["Rust", "Dioxus", "WebAssembly", "libSQL / SQLite", "ONNX Runtime", "Kokoro TTS", "SCORM 1.2/2004", "xAPI", "Stripe", "Playwright", "Hugo", "Netlify"], "metrics": [{"label": "Commits", "value": "926"}, {"label": "Rust code", "value": "79,600 lines"}, {"label": "Rust tests (#[test])", "value": "846"}, {"label": "Crates", "value": "22"}, {"label": "Prototype tests (Lectern, claimed in its README)", "value": "~1,800"}, {"label": "Prototype audit findings (README)", "value": "663 raised, ~661 fixed"}], "status": "Live; web and downloads for Mac, Windows, Linux, plus headless; 7-day trial, from $50/month", "timeline": "Prototype (Lectern) spring 2026; Podium rewrite Oct 2026 (git history 2026-10-23 to 2026-10-27)", "progress": ["Lectern TypeScript prototype: React/Vite editor, player engine, SCORM, xAPI, PPTX and DOCX import, Tauri 2 desktop", "Podium rewrite in Rust: core model, store, auth, player, studio editor, accessibility checker, TTS", "Storyline .story import, Mac/Windows/Linux packages, Stripe billing with Solo and Team plans", "Marketing site, help center and blog live on Netlify", "Built at Jason's employer Grow as his own engineering work; no client data in this sheet"], "dogfooding": ["Jason used it to author food-safety and harassment-prevention training", "Exports tested end to end against an LRS in Playwright (answered questions arrive as cmi.interaction statements, return visits send 'resumed')", "The repo carries its own red-team and black-team pentest reports with a remediation log (RED-TEAM-REMEDIATION.md, BLACK-TEAM-PENTEST.md)"], "links": [{"label": "Live site", "url": "https://podium-elearning.netlify.app/"}], "buildLogTitle": "git history and repo counts, Oct 2026", "buildLog": "$ git log --format='%h %ad %s' -n 14   # Podium\naa37376 2026-10-27 Desktop: the web player that exported courses ship is built into the program (build.r\nbb8baeb 2026-10-26 release: one --package-types per package type\n3724528 2026-10-26 Podium for Windows and Linux: the desktop app keeps its data in the system's app-data\n4208e46 2026-10-26 Billing: back from checkout, and on Refresh, an owner's Billing page has the server a\n763e4d4 2026-10-26 Billing: when Stripe refuses the server's secret key (401/403), checkout and the bill\n6fcf068 2026-10-26 Billing through Stripe: a 7-day free trial (PODIUM_TRIAL_DAYS), then an owner chooses\nd5f7a85 2026-10-26 Teams: an admin turns a member's access off and back on from the Team page, without t\nee28b0c 2026-10-26 Narration plays smoothly in the Mac app: it no longer seeks several times a second (2\ne268018 2026-10-26 studio: an Account page (the avatar menu's Account) where people change their own nam\n820d53a 2026-10-26 Mac app: bundle.sh --install puts Podium.app in /Applications, beside the other apps \n74c0ed9 2026-10-26 Mac app: bundle.sh --install copies Podium.app to ~/Applications and registers it wit\ncd381b5 2026-10-26 Mac app: drag-and-drop interactions and the video and audio blocks' own controls (tra\nb126f48 2026-10-26 Mac app, stage 1: the studio builds for the desktop (cargo build -p podium-studio --n\n30f3d8c 2026-10-26 studio: the Podium mark is the website's (three rising bars on a violet-to-pink squar\n\n$ git log --oneline | wc -l\n926\n$ grep -rE '#\\[(tokio::)?test\\]' crates apps | wc -l\n846\n$ ls crates\na11y api auth core designs docx edit export fonts i18n lectern-import\nplatform player player-ui pptx render scorm store story templates\ntheme-packs tts xapi", "slug": "podium", "n": 5, "platforms": "Web · PC · Mac · Linux · Headless", "category": "B2B", "shots": [{"src": "img/podium-00.jpg", "caption": "Live site, podium-elearning.netlify.app", "w": 1400, "h": 875}, {"src": "img/podium-01.jpg", "caption": "The shapes menu open, showing rectangle, oval, triangle, right triangle, line, arrow and star", "w": 1400, "h": 787}, {"src": "img/podium-02.jpg", "caption": "The Animations panel with entrance effects and the list of effects on this slide, one expanded to show its timing", "w": 1400, "h": 787}, {"src": "img/podium-03.jpg", "caption": "The Assets page: kind filters, a Find by name box, and a list of files, each with its type, size, date added, what uses it, and Open and Delete buttons", "w": 1400, "h": 787}, {"src": "img/podium-04.jpg", "caption": "The Billing page with the trial's days left and the Solo and Team plans", "w": 700, "h": 370}, {"src": "img/podium-05.jpg", "caption": "The Player page with layout presets, colour settings and a live preview", "w": 1400, "h": 787}, {"src": "img/podium-06.jpg", "caption": "The History page listing saved versions, each with Restore", "w": 1400, "h": 787}, {"src": "img/podium-07.jpg", "caption": "The Courses page with three courses listed, and New course, Import course and Import theme buttons", "w": 1400, "h": 787}, {"src": "img/podium-08.jpg", "caption": "The Design panel with the theme library, colourway, slide size, background, theme colours and fonts", "w": 1400, "h": 787}, {"src": "img/podium-09.jpg", "caption": "The Change design dialog with eight layouts on the Title slides tab", "w": 1150, "h": 536}, {"src": "img/podium-10.jpg", "caption": "The canvas in Phone view: the title slide laid out as one tall column inside a phone outline, with notes beside it explaining how the slide reflows", "w": 1400, "h": 787}, {"src": "img/podium-11.jpg", "caption": "Build with Podium", "w": 1400, "h": 875}, {"src": "img/podium-12.jpg", "caption": "Podium vs Articulate 360", "w": 1400, "h": 875}, {"src": "img/podium-13.jpg", "caption": "Podium help", "w": 1400, "h": 875}, {"src": "img/podium-14.jpg", "caption": "Features", "w": 1400, "h": 875}]}, {"name": "Quarry", "tagline": "One board for jobs, clients and the people behind them", "oneLiner": "A web app that finds job openings, prospective clients and the right person at each, drafts the outreach, and tracks every thread to a yes.", "problem": "Finding work means juggling job boards, company research, contact lookups, application forms and follow-ups across many tabs. Quarry puts the hunt, the research and the pipeline in one place and automates the repetitive parts.", "whatItIs": "Quarry has four hunt styles (Clients, Jobs, People, Local) and views that fit each: list, map, kanban board, an outreach queue for phone and email, plus Apply and Inbox for jobs. It adds a Who-is profile lookup, a hiring mode for employers, a small marketplace, a Chrome extension that fills application forms, and a nightly search. It is live as a sales site with accounts, hunt-point credits and Stripe billing, while the app itself runs on Jason's own server.", "howItWorks": ["Dependency-light Node 22 server (TypeScript run natively, no runtime npm dependencies; sass is dev-only), with SCSS compiled to a committed stylesheet that a test checks is current.", "Data layers: read-only adapters over Jason's earlier tools (job-lead-generation for jobs, ClientHunter for client hunts), per-account state folders, and a shared library of public research reused across accounts with hunt points refunded for library hits.", "Pipeline at 2am: pull new postings from board scrapers, ATS feeds, Dice and Himalayas, check small companies' careers pages, search outward from family bases at 25, 50, 100 and 150 miles, then score each job.", "Chrome extension (manifest, background, form scripts) fills Greenhouse application forms and attaches the resume; it never presses Submit and leaves any unanswered placeholder blank.", "Mail tracking reads a plus-addressed Gmail inbox every 30 minutes, read-only, and moves jobs to Applied or Rejected by rules; interview-sounding mail is flagged, not moved.", "Billing and credits: Stripe Checkout, signed-webhook verification, plan points that roll over a month and bought points that never expire. Hosted on a systemd service behind Tailscale with Gatekeep passkey sign-in; the sales site deploys to Netlify from main."], "aiAngle": ["AI is real but one part of a larger product. Prompts go through the local `claude` CLI in print mode on a subscription (Sonnet by default), run from an empty working folder with tools disabled except web search where research needs it.", "Structured output: askJSON forces JSON-only replies and extracts the first balanced object, tolerating chatter and reporting cut-off responses.", "Guardrails enforced in code, not only in prompts: Who-is and hiring lookups keep professional facts with sources, drop candidates sourced only from people-search sites, and strip phone numbers, emails and street addresses from results. Unit tests cover these rules.", "Application policy: short factual answers come only from the user's own facts, essays follow the AI policy stated on each form (draft, refine only, or stay out), and Claude never adds experience the user does not have. Quarry never submits.", "MCP integrations I verified in code: Quarry is itself a client of Clera's MCP server (src/clera.ts, session and protocol-version handling, applies through Clera with a send-only-on-confirm flow), and the job pipeline calls the Dice MCP server (mcp.dice.com). Himalayas is queried through its public API, not MCP.", "Deterministic where a model is not needed: email sorting is rules-based, and the Clera path states no model is involved. Every decision is logged to events.jsonl as a future small-model training set."], "stack": ["TypeScript", "Node 22", "Claude CLI", "MCP", "Chrome extension", "Leaflet", "Stripe", "SCSS", "Tailscale", "Netlify"], "metrics": [{"label": "Commits", "value": "67 in 9 days"}, {"label": "Source lines (TS + JS)", "value": "~6,100"}, {"label": "Tests passing", "value": "18"}, {"label": "Hunt styles", "value": "4"}, {"label": "Jobs tracked (own pipeline)", "value": "371"}, {"label": "People researched", "value": "72"}], "status": "Live sales site; app in active development", "timeline": "Nov 2 – Nov 10, 2026 (as a combined product)", "progress": ["Nov 2026: Quarry merged jobs, clients and people hunting into one tool over earlier job-lead-generation and ClientHunter tools.", "Shipped: map and board views, outreach queue, apply extension, nightly search, Who-is lookups, hiring mode, employer marketplace, shared research library.", "Nov 10: Stripe-backed hunt-point plans and packs, SCSS rebuild, setup wizard, help pages, and a rebuilt animated sales site.", "Next: public deployment beyond the tailnet, and more application forms than Greenhouse."], "dogfooding": ["Jason's own job search runs through it: jobs.json holds 371 jobs and 72 researched contacts, with the nightly search scoring them near his family bases.", "events.jsonl records his real application-prepared events, and the pipeline includes resume variants for roles like Applied AI Engineer and Forward Deployed Engineer.", "Runs on his own server as a systemd service with passkey sign-in, with a separate sandbox and fake Stripe and Clera servers for testing.", "His help docs are generated by AutoDevDocs, which crawls Quarry (Outreach, Apply and the extension are crawl targets). Screenshots on the page show demo data."], "links": [{"label": "Live site", "url": "https://quarry-sales.netlify.app/"}, {"label": "Help docs", "url": "https://quarry-sales.netlify.app/docs/"}], "buildLogTitle": "npm test and recent commits, Nov 2026", "buildLog": "$ npm test\nok 1 - a new month rolls over up to one month of unused plan points\nok 2 - spending takes plan points first, so what can expire is never what was bought\nok 3 - a webhook counts only with a fresh, valid Stripe signature\nok 6 - a failed hunt gives its credits back\nok 10 - a candidate sourced only from people-search sites is dropped\nok 11 - phone numbers, emails and street addresses are taken out of what a search says\nok 14 - you see other accounts' listings, never your own\n# tests 18\n# pass 18\n# fail 0\n\n$ git log --format='%h %ad %s' --date=short | head -6\n5237b12 2026-11-10 Startup line says whether payments are on, and test or live\n49879f6 2026-11-10 Employers get Clients and Hiring only; hunt points follow the tab\nc64e60a 2026-11-10 Buy more hunt points: the paywall, with monthly plans and packs through Stripe\nd82a560 2026-11-10 Merge shared-library: hunt points back for library hits, styles in SCSS\nb6a208a 2026-11-10 Styles are SCSS: one partial per part of the app, shared partials\n9604ec3 2026-11-10 Quarry's library: every account's hunts share website reads, map searches", "slug": "quarry", "n": 6, "platforms": "Web", "category": "B2B · Agents", "shots": [{"src": "img/quarry-00.jpg", "caption": "quarry-sales.netlify.app: the board hero", "w": 1400, "h": 875}, {"src": "img/quarry-01.jpg", "caption": "Jobs board with nightly run, fit scores and distance (demo data)", "w": 1400, "h": 875}, {"src": "img/quarry-02.jpg", "caption": "Local map: clients, jobs and people around a home base", "w": 1400, "h": 875}, {"src": "img/quarry-03.jpg", "caption": "Apply view with the Chrome extension", "w": 1400, "h": 875}, {"src": "img/quarry-04.jpg", "caption": "Clients style on the Outreach view, with the Today queue, a phone number, a Draft an email button, outcome buttons and the call script", "w": 1400, "h": 875}, {"src": "img/quarry-05.jpg", "caption": "Jobs style on the Inbox view, with a drafted email to Jordan Lee and a From employers list showing a confirmation and an interview request", "w": 1400, "h": 875}, {"src": "img/quarry-06.jpg", "caption": "A job on the Through Clera route, showing what Clera filled in, with Fill in again and Send this application buttons", "w": 1400, "h": 875}, {"src": "img/quarry-07.jpg", "caption": "The New search dialog in Clients style", "w": 1092, "h": 620}, {"src": "img/quarry-08.jpg", "caption": "The Who is… dialog with Name, City, Company and What for fields", "w": 1092, "h": 620}, {"src": "img/quarry-09.jpg", "caption": "A Who is… result showing two people named Alex Rivera side by side", "w": 1400, "h": 875}, {"src": "img/quarry-10.jpg", "caption": "Quarry Help", "w": 1400, "h": 875}, {"src": "img/quarry-11.jpg", "caption": "Sign in", "w": 1400, "h": 875}]}, {"name": "BTCSignal.ai", "tagline": "A paper-trading agent that publishes every reason", "oneLiner": "A Bitcoin data and signals site where a rules engine proposes trades, Claude reviews them, and every decision and loss is published, paid for with Lightning.", "problem": "Retail Bitcoin traders mostly choose between expensive institutional data tools and free signals from anonymous accounts that never show their record. The first version of this site published one AI-written daily call. Results were mixed and the method was hard to verify, so it was retired.", "whatItIs": "A Next.js site rebuilt in 2026 around one rule: every number names its source and age. It serves live market, derivatives and on-chain data, 23 tools, free price alerts, a public JSON API, and a trading agent that runs hourly on a paper account. The agent's decisions, reasons, losses and backtest are all on the page, including a backtest that has not yet passed the site's own out-of-sample bar.", "howItWorks": ["A deterministic seven-factor engine (4h trend, daily regime, momentum, RSI, perp funding, Fear & Greed, 30-day momentum) scores the market every hour and either sits out or proposes a swing trade with a 3x daily-ATR trailing stop.", "Hard risk limits run before any model sees the trade: 1% of equity risked per trade, max 50% notional, no leverage, max 3 entries a day, reward:risk of at least 1.5, auto-halt at 15% drawdown.", "Claude reviews each proposal with a structured, schema-validated output (approve, reduce or veto, a size multiplier, public reasoning, up to four key risks). It can only shrink or veto; it cannot enlarge a position, move a stop or open a trade the rules did not propose. Exits never wait on Claude.", "Any review failure is treated as a veto. If no Anthropic credentials are configured, the agent falls back to a labelled rules-only mode instead of pretending a review happened.", "Market data comes from Coinbase, Kraken, OKX, Deribit, Hyperliquid, mempool.space, alternative.me and CoinGecko. The public API returns a Datum with source and fetch time, and returns an error rather than a guessed value when a source fails.", "Backend is Postgres through Drizzle, with schedules run from an always-on server because Vercel Hobby only allows daily crons and GitHub scheduled runs proved unreliable.", "Pro tools are unlocked with Lightning invoices (LNbits), as time-limited passes with no subscription and no account required."], "aiAngle": ["Constrained model authority: the LLM is a risk reviewer with veto and shrink powers only, sitting behind deterministic rules and hard limits. The failure mode of the model is bounded by design.", "Structured outputs: the review is a Zod schema (decision, size multiplier, reasoning, risks) parsed from the model response, so the trade log is machine-checkable.", "Fail-safe behavior: errors veto the trade, and a missing key is shown on the page as 'Rules only (Claude review not configured)'.", "Honest evaluation: a three-year hourly backtest with fees and slippage, a walk-forward out-of-sample run (tune on 270 days, trade the next 90, roll), and an explicit live-trading gate (profit factor at least 1.2, Sharpe at least 0.5, win rate at least 35%).", "A pivot driven by a real lesson: free-form AI daily calls were hard to verify, so the rebuild put the model where its output can be checked and bounded, and put the numbers in code.", "Claude-powered Pro tools (Trade Coach, Setup Grader, chart analysis) return 503 without a key rather than faking output, and AI usage is metered in the database."], "stack": ["Next.js 16", "React 19", "TypeScript", "Postgres", "Drizzle ORM", "Anthropic SDK", "Zod", "Vitest", "Lightning (LNbits)", "Vercel", "lightweight-charts"], "metrics": [{"label": "Tests (Vitest)", "value": "237 passing"}, {"label": "Test files", "value": "29"}, {"label": "App TypeScript", "value": "~19,500 lines"}, {"label": "Tools on site", "value": "23"}, {"label": "Public API endpoints", "value": "6 documented"}, {"label": "Repo commits (both versions)", "value": "1,326"}], "status": "Live, paper trading only", "timeline": "Nov 2025 (v1) – Nov 2026 (rebuild live Nov 3, 2026)", "progress": ["v1 (Hugo and Netlify, Nov 2025) published AI-written daily calls and was retired; its posts remain as a labelled archive.", "Rebuilt in Next.js and cut over on Nov 3, 2026; the old Netlify site now 301-redirects to the new one.", "Agent engine v2.3.0 runs hourly with a public decision log. An earlier intraday config lost 81% in backtest because fees overwhelmed it, and was retired.", "Current live-config backtest (in-sample, chosen after seeing the data): +2.7% over 34 trades versus 120.6% for buy-and-hold. Walk-forward out-of-sample: -5.2%, profit factor 0.65. The live-trading gate is NOT passed, and the site says so.", "Next: pass the out-of-sample gate and 30+ days of consistent paper trading before any real money."], "dogfooding": ["Jason built, deploys and operates it: the agent runs hourly on his always-on server and posts its own decision log (last run was 2026-11-11 when checked).", "The Decision Log shows real agent output, including the repeated 'SHORT signal blocked by risk: shorts disabled (spot venue)'. Nothing is cleaned up.", "The site publishes its own failures: retired v1, the failed out-of-sample gate, and a retired config with -81% backtest return.", "Real Lightning payments are part of the pre-launch checklist in the deploy runbook (buy a day pass with real sats and restore it with the recovery code)."], "links": [{"label": "Live site", "url": "https://btcsignal-ai.vercel.app/"}, {"label": "Trading agent", "url": "https://btcsignal-ai.vercel.app/agent"}, {"label": "About (the rebuild)", "url": "https://btcsignal-ai.vercel.app/about"}, {"label": "Alerts", "url": "https://btcsignal-ai.vercel.app/alerts"}, {"label": "API docs", "url": "https://btcsignal-ai.vercel.app/api-docs"}, {"label": "GitHub", "url": "https://github.com/CaliforniaHodl/BTCSignal_ai"}], "buildLogTitle": "test run and recent commits, Nov 2026", "buildLog": "$ npm test\n RUN  v5.1.0  web/\n\n Test Files  29 passed (29)\n      Tests  237 passed (237)\n   Duration  878ms\n\n$ git log --format='%h %ad %s' --date=short\nb6440b6 2026-11-03 Realistic stop fills in violent candles; run the schedule from an always-on server\n3c4ad05 2026-11-03 gitignore: .env*.local (added by vercel link)\nb5b2d0e 2026-11-03 Deploy: Next.js framework preset; schedules via GitHub Actions\nf7c0367 2026-11-03 Production URL: btcsignal-ai.vercel.app (single SITE_URL source)\n8133bd1 2026-11-03 Add deploy & cutover runbook\nff59732 2026-11-03 Fix mobile overflow: let cards/steps shrink inside grids\ncda9598 2026-11-03 Merge AI & strategy batch; resolve shared-module conflicts\ne3d6b2a 2026-11-03 Markets batch: alerts migration, alerts cron, registry\n7e292ab 2026-11-03 Port AI & strategy tools: trade coach, setup grader, chart analysis", "slug": "btcsignal", "n": 7, "platforms": "Web · API", "category": "Applied AI · Agents", "shots": [{"src": "img/btcsignal-00.jpg", "caption": "Home: live BTC price and signals, each with its source", "w": 1400, "h": 875}, {"src": "img/btcsignal-01.jpg", "caption": "Smart chart: candles with automatic support and resistance levels and RSI", "w": 1400, "h": 620}, {"src": "img/btcsignal-02.jpg", "caption": "About: why the AI-written daily calls were retired", "w": 1400, "h": 875}, {"src": "img/btcsignal-03.jpg", "caption": "The trading agent: rules propose, Claude can approve, shrink or veto, every reason published", "w": 1400, "h": 875}, {"src": "img/btcsignal-04.jpg", "caption": "Alerts: price, funding and sentiment alerts", "w": 1400, "h": 875}, {"src": "img/btcsignal-05.jpg", "caption": "Alpha Radar (Pro), unlocked with a Lightning pass", "w": 1400, "h": 875}, {"src": "img/btcsignal-06.jpg", "caption": "Public API: free, read-only JSON endpoints", "w": 1400, "h": 875}]}, {"name": "Hablo", "tagline": "Learn Spanish by speaking, on-device AI", "oneLiner": "A speaking-first Spanish course for web and Android where speech recognition, the conversation partner and the voice all run on the device.", "problem": "Most language apps are tap-and-match with little real speaking, and the ones with AI conversation depend on a cloud service and an account. Hablo grades what you actually say, word by word, and keeps working offline.", "whatItIs": "Hablo is a course of 24 units and 240 phrases (48 lessons, 25 role-play missions) built around listening, building sentences, saying them aloud, grammar and conversation. Sofia, a local AI partner, talks with you in simple Mexican Spanish while a separate checker quietly corrects your grammar. The first 5 lessons are free; the rest is a one-time purchase. It runs in the browser and as an Android app, with Mac builds from the same codebase.", "howItWorks": ["Speech in: whisper.cpp (small on Mac, base on phone) transcribes the learner; a word-alignment scorer from murmur-core grades it against the target phrase.", "Conversation: Qwen2.5-3B-Instruct (Q4_K_M, about 2 GB) via llama.cpp produces Sofia's replies, streamed, with the KV cache reused across turns.", "Grammar checker is a second pass whose output is forced to valid JSON with a GBNF grammar, so the UI can rely on its shape.", "Speech out: sherpa-onnx TTS (Piper es_MX voice for Spanish, Kokoro for English tips); audio starts playing sentence by sentence while the model is still generating.", "Tauri 2 + Svelte 5 + Rust app; the same SvelteKit build is served as a static web app, with models downloaded from Hugging Face into the browser.", "whisper.cpp and llama.cpp both bundle ggml, so a patched whisper-rs-sys links whisper against llama.cpp's ggml to avoid the two copies corrupting each other.", "Android: signed APK via NDK scripts, evening and come-back reminders scheduled on the phone, Play Billing for purchases."], "aiAngle": ["Small on-device LLM chosen for a hard constraint: nothing leaves the device, so the model must fit a phone and respond fast.", "Constrained decoding: GBNF grammar forces the grammar-check output into parseable JSON instead of relying on prompt-only formatting.", "Latency design: streamed generation, KV cache reuse per turn, and sentence-level TTS so speech starts before the reply is finished.", "Separation of roles: the conversation model stays in character while a separate checker call handles corrections.", "Evaluation tooling: a course_check example synthesizes every phrase with the app's own voice and checks whisper can recognise it; a selftest runs the whisper, LLM and TTS round trip headless, including on a phone via adb.", "Scoring is deterministic and tested (word alignment, digits read as Spanish words, un/una/uno matching)."], "stack": ["Tauri 2", "Svelte 5", "Rust", "llama.cpp", "Qwen2.5-3B", "whisper.cpp", "sherpa-onnx (Piper, Kokoro)", "Android NDK", "Hugo", "Netlify", "Lemon Squeezy"], "metrics": [{"label": "Units / phrases", "value": "24 / 240"}, {"label": "Role-play missions", "value": "25"}, {"label": "Commits (app)", "value": "39"}, {"label": "Source LOC (TS, Svelte, Rust)", "value": "~9,400"}, {"label": "Platforms", "value": "Web, Android"}, {"label": "Free lessons", "value": "5"}], "status": "Live at v0.8.0 (web), Android build", "timeline": "Oct 15, 2026 – Nov 2026", "progress": ["Course built from a single course.json with generated lessons, exercises and recorded phrase audio.", "Grammar tab (conjugation, tu/usted, tense choice) and unlockable missions shipped Oct 16.", "Browser version with desktop layout shipped Oct 17.", "v0.8.0 paywall release Oct 24: 5 free lessons, a 10-lesson pack or the full app, license-key activation on web and Play Billing on Android.", "Hugo marketing site live on Netlify; a YouTube Short ad concept exists.", "Next: Mac and Android store download links (currently shown as coming soon on the site)."], "dogfooding": ["Reuses Jason's own murmur crate for transcription and scoring.", "Phrase audio is rendered with the app's own Spanish voice and checked by whisper, a self-test loop run on the course content.", "Jason built and tested the Android APK on a USB-connected phone with the repo's own scripts."], "links": [{"label": "Live site", "url": "https://hablo-learn-spanish.netlify.app/"}, {"label": "Web app", "url": "https://hablos.netlify.app/"}, {"label": "YouTube ad concept", "url": "https://www.youtube.com/shorts/XoAjrnT055w"}], "buildLogTitle": "git log, hablo (app)", "buildLog": "544e7db 2026-11-02 Merge pull request #1 from mycelium/fix-202611030328\nbf53de0 2026-11-02 mycelium: upgrade 5 vulnerable package(s)\n9d0d227 2026-10-24 Cargo.lock for 0.8.0\ne8fa4a5 2026-10-24 0.8.0: the paywall release\n36ef043 2026-10-24 Paywall: 5 free lessons, then a 10-lesson pack or the full app\n1b0cc23 2026-10-17 Run hablo in the browser, with a desktop layout\n0942ebc 2026-10-16 Sofia checkpoints are optional: a unit's lessons open the next unit\neab5d21 2026-10-16 Unlock grammar sets and missions when a unit's lessons are done\n70742a3 2026-10-16 Gramatica tab: conjugation, tu/usted/slang, and choosing the tense\n\n# test suites in repo\n# node --test: streak (16), shop (16), drill (8), grammar (2) cases\n# cargo test: murmur-core drill scorer, tts language splitting\n# tests/ui-smoke.mjs: whole UI against a mocked backend\n$ curl -sI https://hablo-learn-spanish.netlify.app/ | head -1\nHTTP/2 200", "slug": "hablo", "n": 8, "platforms": "Web · Android", "category": "Applied AI · Voice", "shots": [{"src": "img/hablo-00.jpg", "caption": "The learning path: units, the daily goal, and missions with Sofía", "w": 1400, "h": 674}, {"src": "img/hablo-01.jpg", "caption": "A lesson: hear the phrase, pick what it means", "w": 1400, "h": 674}, {"src": "img/hablo-02.jpg", "caption": "Instant feedback after checking an answer", "w": 1400, "h": 674}, {"src": "img/hablo-03.jpg", "caption": "Misiones: 25 role-play conversations with Sofía, unlocked unit by unit", "w": 1400, "h": 674}, {"src": "img/hablo-04.jpg", "caption": "Gramática: conjugation, tú vs. usted vs. slang, and tense choice", "w": 1400, "h": 674}, {"src": "img/hablo-05.jpg", "caption": "Charla libre: Sofía, the on-device conversation partner (Mac and Android)", "w": 1400, "h": 674}, {"src": "img/hablo-06.jpg", "caption": "Landing page: say-it-aloud exercise graded word by word", "w": 1400, "h": 875}, {"src": "img/hablo-07.jpg", "caption": "Full landing page: how a lesson works, features, pricing", "w": 1400, "h": 2200}, {"src": "img/hablo-08.jpg", "caption": "The web app: Chispa, the guide, on first launch", "w": 1400, "h": 875}]}, {"name": "Writepad", "tagline": "Markdown studio for scripts, songs and books", "oneLiner": "A local-first Markdown publishing studio for writers, with A/B drafts, dictation, narration and a headless MCP server so AI agents can edit alongside you.", "problem": "Writers revising a script or book juggle copies of files, lose track of which version said what, and have no good way to hear the text back. Existing tools either lock files into a database or leave versioning to the writer.", "whatItIs": "Writepad is a Tauri 2 desktop app (Mac, Windows, Linux) plus a headless CLI. Files are plain Markdown in a folder you choose. It keeps every version of every scene, lays one over another like onion skin, reads text back with a local voice, takes dictation, and exports EPUB and audiobooks. It includes worldbuilding notes, a timeline view and a library that measures the books you learn from. The app is free and open source; EPUB and audiobook export is a $5 license-key unlock.", "howItWorks": ["Rust workspace of three crates (core, cli, devserver) plus the Tauri shell; core holds manuscripts, vault, diff, history, search, comments, EPUB, PDF and narration code.", "Takes (A/B versions) are first-class: fork, compare with word-level diff, merge words from other takes, name cuts and set the live version.", "Vault is a folder of Markdown with optional git-backed history, so work stays portable and diffable.", "Narration: local Kokoro TTS through a Python worker the app installs itself; dictation builds on the murmur crate (local whisper).", "`writepad mcp` is an MCP server over stdio exposing the vault as tools; each agent session runs its own process and they coordinate through file locks.", "Signed auto-updater via GitHub Releases, Lemon Squeezy license activation (works offline afterwards), Hugo marketing site on Netlify with a Zero Trust Analytics visit counter."], "aiAngle": ["MCP server with 30+ tools (list_manuscripts, outline, read, fork, edit, compare, comment, snapshot, history, export and more) so Claude and other agents can work in a writer's vault.", "Guardrails enforced in the tools: an agent only writes takes it created, every take carries its agent's name (shown with a robot marker in the app), and comments are editorial notes that never edit the text.", "Concurrency design: 5 to 20 agents can run at once, each as its own process, coordinated by vault file locks rather than a central server.", "The app watches the vault and shows agent takes and edits live as they land, so a human reviews AI changes as versions, not overwrites.", "Optional 'Polish with Claude' pass on dictation; local-first for everything else.", "Same tools are callable from a shell (`writepad call outline ...`), which makes agent behaviour scriptable and testable."], "stack": ["Rust", "Tauri 2", "CodeMirror 6", "TypeScript", "MCP (stdio)", "Kokoro TTS", "whisper.cpp (via murmur)", "Hugo", "Netlify", "GitHub Actions", "Lemon Squeezy"], "metrics": [{"label": "Commits", "value": "29"}, {"label": "Source LOC (Rust + TS)", "value": "~17,000"}, {"label": "MCP / CLI tools", "value": "30+"}, {"label": "Releases tagged", "value": "v0.1.x"}, {"label": "Platforms", "value": "Mac, Windows, Linux, headless"}, {"label": "Rust unit tests", "value": "56"}], "status": "Shipping: public releases and live site, pre-launch go-to-market", "timeline": "Oct 21, 2026 – Nov 2026", "progress": ["Signed self-updating installers for Mac (Apple Silicon and Intel) and Windows; Linux packages added to the release workflow.", "Shipped: onion-skin versions, merge versions, cuts, timeline, world notes, live-preview Markdown editing, history, EPUB and audiobook export.", "$5 Publish unlock via Lemon Squeezy license keys, with offline operation after activation.", "Demo vault (The Monkey's Paw, public domain) shipped with the repo and releases.", "Open items in GTM.md: custom domain, Mac and Windows signing, launch posts."], "dogfooding": ["Its demo vault is a real multi-version revision of a public-domain story, with cuts, timelines and a cast, used to exercise every feature.", "Uses Jason's own murmur for dictation and Zero Trust Analytics for visit counting.", "Help pages generated with Jason's AutoDevDocs, as with most of his projects.", "Dependency fixes land through his own Mycelium security tooling (several mycelium PRs in the log)."], "links": [{"label": "Live site", "url": "https://writepad-app.netlify.app/"}, {"label": "Source and releases", "url": "https://github.com/jasonsutter87/writepad"}], "buildLogTitle": "git log, writepad", "buildLog": "59f2167 2026-11-03 deps: replace deprecated serde_yaml with serde_norway (#24)\n3fe280f 2026-11-03 deps: upgrade zip to 9.3 (clears 7 critical advisories) (#23)\na7228d5 2026-11-02 mycelium: upgrade 14 vulnerable package(s) (#22)\n1e41fb1 2026-11-02 Sitemap: give every page a priority so none drop out (#21)\n2172971 2026-10-31 fix: double-click a version chip to rename it (#18)\n57ed3aa 2026-10-31 Add Zero Trust Analytics visit counting (#15)\nb224c9c 2026-10-31 Merge versions: pick words from other takes into a new one (#16)\nd2525b4 2026-10-29 Publish unlock (#14)\n2875070 2026-10-29 Live preview editing: headings, lists, checklists, quotes, links and pictures (#13)\n\n$ writepad tools   # MCP / CLI tool names (excerpt)\nlist_manuscripts outline read fork edit write compare set_live\nadd_block search list_notes read_note write_note append_note\nsnapshot history export import comment comments leave_out\ntimeline timeline_add scene_cast set_props set_target profile", "slug": "writepad", "n": 9, "platforms": "Mac · PC · Linux · Headless", "category": "Applied AI · Voice", "shots": [{"src": "img/writepad-00.jpg", "caption": "Live site: onion-skinned takes and Listen", "w": 1400, "h": 875}, {"src": "img/writepad-01.jpg", "caption": "Desk: take diff, cast, cuts, A/B listen", "w": 1400, "h": 875}, {"src": "img/writepad-02.jpg", "caption": "The Export dialog with every format", "w": 1040, "h": 972}, {"src": "img/writepad-03.jpg", "caption": "The Library comparison: your manuscript against the target, measure by measure", "w": 1400, "h": 875}, {"src": "img/writepad-04.jpg", "caption": "The Your target dialog", "w": 1040, "h": 900}, {"src": "img/writepad-05.jpg", "caption": "A page with POV and setting properties under its title", "w": 1400, "h": 287}, {"src": "img/writepad-06.jpg", "caption": "The Add a screenplay dialog", "w": 1040, "h": 512}, {"src": "img/writepad-07.jpg", "caption": "Search results for a phrase, with matches highlighted", "w": 1400, "h": 875}, {"src": "img/writepad-08.jpg", "caption": "The timeline list with events, scene chips and told-earlier flags", "w": 1400, "h": 875}, {"src": "img/writepad-09.jpg", "caption": "The Story vs telling chart with crossing lines for events told out of order", "w": 1400, "h": 875}, {"src": "img/writepad-10.jpg", "caption": "Two versions of The visitor above the text, with + Fork and Delete", "w": 1400, "h": 283}, {"src": "img/writepad-11.jpg", "caption": "WRITEpad with the demo vault open, showing the Manuscripts tab", "w": 1400, "h": 875}, {"src": "img/writepad-12.jpg", "caption": "WRITEpad help", "w": 1400, "h": 875}, {"src": "img/writepad-13.jpg", "caption": "Download WRITEpad for Mac and Windows", "w": 1400, "h": 875}, {"src": "img/writepad-14.jpg", "caption": "Pricing", "w": 1400, "h": 875}]}, {"name": "TallyBook", "tagline": "QuickBooks plus TurboTax for individuals, on your computer", "oneLiner": "A local-first bookkeeping, budgeting and basic tax-prep app for freelancers, small businesses and households, on Mac, Windows, Linux and headless.", "problem": "Small businesses and households juggle spreadsheets, a bookkeeping subscription and a separate tax tool, then hand the CPA a pile to sort. Bank data ends up uploaded to someone else's cloud.", "whatItIs": "TallyBook reads and categorizes bank transactions, keeps real double-entry books in a single SQLite file the user owns, and prepares Schedule C worksheets, quarterly estimates and 1099 tracking so there is less for the accountant to sort. A household budget is free; yearly plans add bank sync and phone app ($20), a business side ($49) and tax prep ($75).", "howItWorks": ["Rust workspace of 9 crates: tally-core (money, double-entry journal, budgets, rules, tax), tally-store (SQLite), tally-feed (SimpleFIN, OFX/QFX, CSV), tally-api, tally-sync, tally-connect, tally-calendar, tally-mcp, tally-devserver", "Desktop app is Tauri with a React/TypeScript UI; the same API layer can run without a window as a headless server", "Imported bank lines go to a Review inbox with a suggestion: a match to an existing entry, a user rule (contains / equals / starts-with, direction, priority), or the user's last choice; accept one or all, with undo", "Recurring-payment detection scans imported history and offers rent, subscriptions and allowances as bills in one click; dismissed ones are remembered", "Tax center maps categories to Schedule C lines (meals at 50%, home office), computes quarterly estimates with the IRS safe-harbor rule, tracks 1099s and leaves card payments out", "Books are one documented .tallybook file; optional whole-file encryption with a 256-bit key in the OS keychain, and only encrypted copies can go to cloud folders or Git", "Mistakes are voided, never deleted, and past periods can be locked after filing"], "aiAngle": ["Ships its own MCP server (tally-mcp, 19 tools, hand-written JSON-RPC over stdio, no SDK dependency): `tallybook-desktop --mcp` lets Claude, Codex or any MCP client query the books", "Read-only by default; `--write` is an explicit opt-in that unlocks categorizing the review inbox, recording cash and setting budgets, and write tools are hidden from tools/list otherwise", "Agent calls go through the same dispatcher as the desktop UI, so an agent gets the same validation, undo and audit trail as a person", "Smart categorization is deterministic and explainable: rules, learned user choices and duplicate detection, with every suggestion reviewable and undoable", "Privacy design: no telemetry, bank credentials only in the OS keychain, nothing uploaded by the app; docs/AGENTS.md documents setup for Claude Code, Claude Desktop and the Agent SDK"], "stack": ["Rust", "Tauri", "React", "TypeScript", "SQLite", "MCP", "SimpleFIN", "Playwright", "Hugo", "Netlify"], "metrics": [{"label": "Commits", "value": "580"}, {"label": "Rust code", "value": "28,500 lines"}, {"label": "TypeScript", "value": "19,400 lines"}, {"label": "Rust tests (#[test])", "value": "340"}, {"label": "Playwright e2e tests", "value": "73"}, {"label": "Rust crates", "value": "9"}], "status": "Live, shipping downloads for Mac, Windows and Linux; paid plans on", "timeline": "Oct 2026 – Nov 2026 (first commit 2026-10-15)", "progress": ["Household budget, bills, goals, bank-file import, reports and encryption free for good", "Bank sync through SimpleFIN, invoices, bills, P&L, balance sheet", "Schedule C worksheet, quarterly estimates, 1099 tracking and an accountant export package", "Google Calendar sync, encrypted cloud copies and phone sync; import from the Ledger app", "Next: direct QuickBooks import (on the wishlist); sibling app Tallie (personal finance) already forks the engine"], "dogfooding": ["Jason's own app: the website, help center and blog ship from the same repo and deploy to Netlify", "Mycelium dependency scans have opened and merged security-fix PRs against it (several in the last two weeks of history)", "Zero Trust Analytics, another of his products, is wired into the site for visit counting", "Its engine is reused by a second product, Tallie, which shares tally-core and forks tally-store"], "links": [{"label": "Live site", "url": "https://tallybook-app.netlify.app/"}, {"label": "Download", "url": "https://tallybook-app.netlify.app/download/"}], "buildLogTitle": "git history and repo counts, Nov 2026", "buildLog": "$ git log --oneline -n 12   # tallybook\n3b2a997 2026-11-03 Merge pull request #45 from jasonsutter87/calendar-sync\n04856f4 2026-11-03 Merge pull request #44 from jasonsutter87/mycelium/fix-202611032257\nb12e220 2026-11-03 mycelium: upgrade 1 vulnerable package(s)\n329c1d5 2026-11-03 Merge pull request #42 from jasonsutter87/calendar-sync\n17d97b3 2026-11-03 fix(app): drop minimatch@^3 override that crashed ESLint\ncedce67 2026-11-03 Merge pull request #43 from jasonsutter87/mycelium/fix-parent-202611030947-tauri-\ndac960e 2026-11-03 mycelium: bump tauri-plugin-updater to clear critical zip advisories\n16e154f 2026-11-02 Merge pull request #41 from jasonsutter87/mycelium/fix-202611030506\neef644b 2026-11-02 mycelium: upgrade 15 vulnerable package(s)\ne44ffb1 2026-11-02 Merge pull request #40 from jasonsutter87/sitemap-priority\n9c6460f 2026-11-02 Sitemap: give every page a priority so none drop out\na79f53c 2026-11-02 Merge pull request #39 from jasonsutter87/mycelium/fix-202611022300\n\n$ git log --oneline | wc -l\n580\n$ grep -rE '#\\[(tokio::)?test\\]' crates app/src-tauri | wc -l\n340\n$ ls crates\ntally-api tally-calendar tally-connect tally-core tally-devserver\ntally-feed tally-mcp tally-store tally-sync", "slug": "tallybook", "n": 10, "platforms": "Mac · PC · Linux · Headless", "category": "B2C", "shots": [{"src": "img/tallybook-00.jpg", "caption": "Live site, tallybook-app.netlify.app", "w": 1400, "h": 875}, {"src": "img/tallybook-01.jpg", "caption": "Review inbox: suggested categories, accept or undo", "w": 1400, "h": 875}, {"src": "img/tallybook-02.jpg", "caption": "Tax center: Schedule C, estimates, 1099s", "w": 1400, "h": 875}, {"src": "img/tallybook-03.jpg", "caption": "The Connect a bank window, which sends you to your bank's sign-in page on Plaid", "w": 1400, "h": 875}, {"src": "img/tallybook-04.jpg", "caption": "Import a statement: drop a CSV, OFX or QFX file and pick the account it's for", "w": 1224, "h": 920}, {"src": "img/tallybook-05.jpg", "caption": "Vendor bills: to pay, overdue, due soon and paid totals, and the list of bills", "w": 1400, "h": 875}, {"src": "img/tallybook-06.jpg", "caption": "Enter a bill: vendor, bill number, dates and terms, lines with category and amount", "w": 1400, "h": 1218}, {"src": "img/tallybook-07.jpg", "caption": "Pay bills: the vendor's unpaid bills, the account to pay from, date, method and total", "w": 1304, "h": 1620}, {"src": "img/tallybook-08.jpg", "caption": "New goal: what it's for, how much, by when, the account it's kept in, and what's already saved", "w": 1064, "h": 888}, {"src": "img/tallybook-09.jpg", "caption": "Home: cash on hand, what needs attention, money in and out, cash flow and where money went", "w": 1400, "h": 875}, {"src": "img/tallybook-10.jpg", "caption": "Add a bill: name, amount, category, how often, due day, account, person and bank description", "w": 1064, "h": 1492}, {"src": "img/tallybook-11.jpg", "caption": "Invoices: unpaid, overdue, paid and draft totals, and the list of invoices with their status", "w": 1400, "h": 875}, {"src": "img/tallybook-12.jpg", "caption": "A new invoice: customer, dates and terms, line items, message and totals", "w": 1400, "h": 875}, {"src": "img/tallybook-13.jpg", "caption": "Receive payment: customer, amount, date, deposit account, method and reference", "w": 1304, "h": 1130}, {"src": "img/tallybook-14.jpg", "caption": "Log cash: sale, expense or deposit, amount, category, account, date, payee and note", "w": 1064, "h": 1408}]}, {"name": "Murmur", "tagline": "Hold a key, talk, clean text lands anywhere", "oneLiner": "A local push-to-talk dictation app for macOS that pastes cleaned-up text into whatever app has focus.", "problem": "Typing is slower than talking, but most dictation tools are cloud-first, tied to one app, or leave filler words and false starts in the text. Jason wanted hold-to-talk dictation that works in a terminal, Slack or a browser and keeps audio on the machine.", "whatItIs": "Murmur is a Rust menu-bar app. Hold Option+Space, speak, release, and the transcript is pasted at the cursor. Transcription runs locally with whisper.cpp on Metal. An optional Claude pass removes fillers and applies spoken self-corrections. The audio, transcription and drill-scoring code lives in a shared crate, murmur-core, which Jason reuses in his other apps.", "howItWorks": ["Pipeline: global hotkey, mic capture (cpal), whisper.cpp with Metal acceleration, optional Claude cleanup, then clipboard paste (enigo) into the focused app.", "Multilingual by default: whisper 'small' model with per-clip language detection, or pin a language via MURMUR_LANGUAGE for short-clip accuracy. Models are selectable (base, medium, large-v3-turbo).", "Model files download on first run to the app data folder; silence is skipped and noise tags are stripped before text is returned.", "Vendored, patched whisper-rs-sys so whisper.cpp can share a single ggml build with llama.cpp when linked into hablo (two ggml copies in one binary corrupt each other).", "murmur-core also holds streaming playback (push audio while it plays) and a word-alignment scorer used by hablo to grade spoken Spanish.", "Packaged as a macOS .app via packaging/bundle.sh; menu-bar icon shows idle, recording and processing states."], "aiAngle": ["Local speech recognition: whisper.cpp with Metal, no audio leaves the machine unless the optional cleanup is on.", "LLM cleanup prompt is written defensively: the transcript is wrapped in tags and treated as data, so a dictated 'write an email' is cleaned, not obeyed (prompt-injection guard).", "Cleanup preserves language and code-switching (Spanish stays Spanish, mixed stays mixed) and never translates or summarizes.", "Graceful degradation: no API key or a failed call falls back to the raw transcript; 15 second request timeout.", "Model choice is a config switch (MURMUR_CLAUDE_MODEL) so latency and cost can be traded per use."], "stack": ["Rust", "whisper.cpp", "Metal", "cpal", "Claude API", "tao / tray-icon", "Cargo workspace"], "metrics": [{"label": "Commits", "value": "7"}, {"label": "Rust LOC (app + core)", "value": "~1,100"}, {"label": "Crates", "value": "2 (app + core)"}, {"label": "Reused by", "value": "hablo, writepad"}], "status": "Working daily tool; core crate reused by two other apps", "timeline": "Oct 2026 – Nov 2026", "progress": ["Oct 15, 2026: first working hold-to-talk build with local whisper and .app bundle.", "Same day: shared murmur-core crate with playback, downloads and scoring; patched whisper-rs-sys.", "Streaming speaker output added so hablo can speak while the model is still writing.", "Nov 2026: automated dependency security upgrade merged via PR.", "Roadmap: per-app modes (literal in terminal, formal in email), personal dictionary, configurable hotkey, iOS/Android shared Rust core."], "dogfooding": ["Writepad's dictation feature builds directly against murmur.", "Hablo shares murmur's model folder and its core crate for speech-to-text and speech scoring.", "Spanish and mixed-language handling exists because Jason dictates in both."], "links": [{"label": "Source", "url": "https://github.com/jasonsutter87/murmur"}], "buildLogTitle": "git log, murmur", "buildLog": "f6ecb5f 2026-11-02 Merge pull request #1 from mycelium/fix-202611030334\n1bb0f70 2026-11-02 mycelium: upgrade 3 vulnerable package(s)\n503ac95 2026-10-15 drill: read digits up to 100 as Spanish words; digit 1 matches un/una/uno\nf741f0e 2026-10-15 core: streaming Speaker/Sink output - push audio while it plays\nd5c53a6 2026-10-15 murmur-core: ureq downloads (no Android TLS panic), skip silence, strip noise tags\nfc382cf 2026-10-15 murmur-core: playback, downloads, same_words; patched whisper-rs-sys\n301dd70 2026-10-15 murmur: hold-to-talk dictation with local whisper, multilingual, .app bundle\n\n$ git show --stat 301dd70\n core/Cargo.toml              |   18 +\n core/examples/drill_check.rs |   21 +\n core/src/audio.rs            |   97 +\n core/src/drill.rs            |  220 +++\n core/src/lib.rs              |    5 +\n core/src/transcribe.rs       |   64 +\n packaging/Info.plist         |   18 +\n packaging/bundle.sh          |   16 +\n src/cleanup.rs               |   86 +\n src/insert.rs                |   33 +\n src/main.rs                  |  215 +++\n 15 files changed, 4865 insertions(+)", "slug": "murmur", "n": 11, "platforms": "Mac · PC", "category": "Applied AI · Voice", "shots": []}, {"name": "Unfold", "tagline": "Recursive LLM pipelines defined as YAML recipes", "oneLiner": "A generation engine where a recipe is a stack of prompt levels and every node expands into the next level, saved to disk after each model call so runs can be resumed, rewritten and hand-edited.", "problem": "Long-form LLM work (a novel, an album, a course) breaks in the middle: a call fails, one section comes out wrong, or the model forgets what came before. Jason had hand-coded this same tree-shaped pipeline four times in a worship-song generator, so he rebuilt it once as a general engine.", "whatItIs": "Unfold walks a tree. Each level of a recipe has a system prompt, an instruction and an output kind (list, json or text). A node, plus all of its ancestors, goes through the next level's prompt and becomes that level's children. Nine packaged recipes cover world bibles, screenplays, novels, songs, albums, courses and flight-training syllabi, and a user can add their own YAML. It runs as a CLI, a TypeScript library and a local HTTP API with a single-file web UI.", "howItWorks": ["Recipes are data: YAML levels with templated prompts ({{path}} only, no logic), `count`, `summary` templates and per-level context rules (all ancestors, parent only, or none).", "The engine builds each request from inputs under headings, labelled ancestors, the tail of the previous sibling (continuity), existing siblings when extending, and an optional steering note. `unfold prompt <run> <node>` prints the exact prompt that would be sent.", "Every model call ends with the tree saved to runs/<id>/tree.json. A failed branch is logged and skipped, and `unfold run` retries only what is missing. Node ids are paths (2.3.1), and remove renumbers siblings.", "Three backends behind one interface: `claude -p` (rides a Claude subscription), the Anthropic SDK (streaming, configurable model and effort), and a deterministic mock used by all tests and dry runs.", "A forgiving parser pulls JSON out of prose and code fences and trims to the requested count, with a line-based fallback for string lists.", "Monorepo of six npm workspace packages: a content-free core plus world, story, song and learn plugins that declare `requires` on each other. A test asserts the core never writes into a package.", "Local HTTP API with background jobs (202, 409 if a run is busy), Server-Sent Events for progress, and host/origin checks, JSON-only bodies and a 127.0.0.1 bind because anything that can reach it can spend tokens."], "aiAngle": ["Prompt design as data: all prompt wording lives in YAML or markdown files, and the engine only adds generic structure, so recipes can be tuned without code changes.", "Context engineering: ancestor inheritance, continuity windows (`continuity_chars`), and sibling awareness so list items and variations take different angles instead of repeating.", "Failure handling for long chains: save after every call, skip-and-retry branches, partial regeneration (`regen`, `extend`, `edit`) with a steering note, and tolerant output parsing.", "Human-in-the-loop editing: hand-edit any node, then keep generating below it. A canon-decisions input lets a human override facts that earlier generations got wrong.", "Domain guardrails in prompts: the flight-training recipe cites ACS tasks and leaves `per POH: ___` blanks instead of inventing tolerances or V-speeds; song style presets never name artists.", "Grounding: song recipes run the model inside a folder containing the World English Bible text so scripture references can be read rather than recalled."], "stack": ["TypeScript", "Node 20+", "YAML", "Anthropic SDK", "Claude CLI", "Vitest", "npm workspaces", "Server-Sent Events"], "metrics": [{"label": "Recipes shipped", "value": "9"}, {"label": "Packages", "value": "6"}, {"label": "Tests (mock backend)", "value": "27 passing"}, {"label": "Source lines (TS + UI)", "value": "~2,800"}, {"label": "Real runs on disk", "value": "11"}, {"label": "Largest run", "value": "90-node novella"}], "status": "Working locally, in personal use; not yet published to npm", "timeline": "Oct 2026 – present", "progress": ["Oct 20, 2026: engine, six packages, CLI, HTTP API and web UI rebuilt from a hand-coded predecessor, with three commits that day.", "Added sideways growth (`extend`: more people, locations and relationships with follow-ups).", "Added a Christian/scripture-based toggle to song recipes in place of a numeric scripture ratio.", "Next (per README): publish packages to npm; the novella recipe lives in the workspace and could move into a package."], "dogfooding": ["runs/ holds 11 real runs from Oct 20 to Oct 31, 2026: two worlds, a screenplay, story arcs, songs, a 10-song album and two novella runs.", "The novella run turns that album into a 10-chapter, 90-node book (chapters, scenes, 600 to 1000 word prose scenes) with the song lyrics held as canon.", "Used on Jason's own song and album projects, with outputs exported as Suno-ready text.", "The UI screenshot is the local web UI open on the real novella run."], "links": [], "buildLogTitle": "npm test, Nov 2026 (mock backend, no network)", "buildLog": "$ npx vitest run --reporter=verbose\n ✓ workspace > findWorkspace > walks up from a subfolder to the nearest unfold.yaml\n ✓ workspace > plugins > pull in their requirements once, dependencies first\n ✓ workspace > plugins > a workspace recipe overrides the packaged one with the same id\n ✓ workspace > plugins > requests carry the recipe's own packs/ as cwd\n ✓ workspace > saving > writes library entries to the workspace, never into a package\n ✓ engine > recipes > all load and validate\n ✓ engine > screenplay tree > unfolds beats > scene-lines > pages with inherited context\n ✓ engine > screenplay tree > regenerates one list item in place and drops its subtree\n ✓ engine > screenplay tree > is resumable after a failure and never redoes finished nodes\n ✓ engine > songs > album = theses fan out, then one song per thesis\n ✓ engine > songs > rejects unknown library keys and inputs up front\n ✓ engine > songs > story arc: eras > theses > songs, saved onto the persona\n ✓ engine > world builder > runs the five-layer cascade, saves to the library\n ✓ engine > parseOutput > finds JSON inside prose and fences, and trims to count\n ✓ engine > growing a tree sideways > adds people and then their relationships\n ✓ engine > growing a tree sideways > adds more children and removes with renumbering\n ✓ server > headless API > creates a run, fills it in the background, edits, regenerates and exports\n ✓ server > headless API > rejects foreign origins, non-JSON bodies and path traversal\n\n Test Files  3 passed (3)\n      Tests  27 passed (27)\n   Duration  1.01s", "slug": "unfold", "n": 12, "platforms": "CLI · Library · HTTP API", "category": "Applied AI · Pipelines", "shots": [{"src": "img/unfold-00.jpg", "caption": "Web UI on a real 90-node novella run: tree, node actions, guidance box", "w": 1400, "h": 875}]}, {"name": "PFM (.pfm)", "tagline": "A universal container format for AI agent output", "oneLiner": "An open file format, with Python and JavaScript libraries, that keeps an agent's output, the conversation behind it, its tool calls and its integrity data in one indexed text file.", "problem": "Every agent tool writes a different thing: markdown blobs, JSON logs, framework-specific traces. None of them reliably record what was produced, how, with which tools, or whether it was tampered with. Sharing, auditing or training on agent output means writing a new parser each time.", "whatItIs": "A UTF-8 text format (#!PFM/1.0) with a metadata block, a byte-offset index, and named sections such as content, chain, tools and reasoning. It ships as a Python package and CLI, a JavaScript/TypeScript parser on npm, a browser viewer and converter, a Chrome extension for capturing chats, a VS Code extension, and two Claude Code skills (capture and recall).", "howItWorks": ["File layout: magic line, #@meta (id, agent, model, timestamp, SHA-256 checksum), #@index of section name, byte offset and length, then content sections and an #!END marker. Files are identified from the first 64 bytes.", "A two-pass writer computes all offsets, so a reader can seek straight to a section without parsing the rest. A lazy reader only loads the sections requested.", "Integrity: SHA-256 checksum over content, HMAC-SHA256 signing with constant-time verification, and AES-256-GCM encryption with PBKDF2 (600k iterations) for encrypted files.", "Converters round-trip to and from JSON, CSV, TXT and Markdown, and pfm merge combines many files into one with each conversation as a section.", "pfm export turns conversation files into fine-tuning JSONL in OpenAI, Alpaca or ShareGPT shape.", "A shared conformance suite checks that the Python and JavaScript implementations agree on the spec.", "The web viewer at getpfm.io is client-side only: files are parsed in the browser and never uploaded."], "aiAngle": ["Provenance for agent output: which agent and model produced it, the full prompt chain, the tool calls, and a checksum or signature to prove it was not edited.", "Training-data pipeline: conversations archived as .pfm export directly to SFT-style JSONL, which closes the loop from agent logs to fine-tuning data.", "Built for agent workflows: the capture and recall Claude Code skills save insights and full sessions to .pfm incrementally, so they survive context compaction.", "Security treated as part of the format: signing and encryption are first-class, and the parser was put through red-team, chaos and APT-simulation assessments (reports are in the repo).", "Used as the archive layer of Jason's own local-first agent system, Werbos, which writes every chat session as a .pfm file."], "stack": ["Python 3.11+", "TypeScript", "Node.js", "AES-256-GCM", "HMAC-SHA256", "Chrome extension (MV3)", "VS Code extension", "npm", "PyPI"], "metrics": [{"label": "Tests", "value": "224 passing"}, {"label": "Python tests", "value": "169"}, {"label": "JS tests", "value": "55"}, {"label": "Commits", "value": "74"}, {"label": "Published releases", "value": "12 on PyPI (0.1.0 to 0.2.1)"}, {"label": "Current version", "value": "0.2.1 (npm and PyPI)"}], "status": "Published and in use", "timeline": "Feb 2026 – Nov 2026", "progress": ["First commit Feb 16, 2026; 0.2.0 added fine-tuning export, 0.2.1 added merge and hardened it (Feb 21).", "Published to npm as get-pfm with provenance via GitHub Actions, and to PyPI as get-pfm.", "Web viewer and converter live at getpfm.io; Chrome extension built and submitted for review (not yet confirmed live in the store).", "Apr 2026: wrote a tiered archival design (hot, warm and cold tiers using pfm merge) for large conversation archives. Marked design only, not yet implemented.", "Nov 2026: dependency security upgrades merged via PR."], "dogfooding": ["Werbos, Jason's local-first agent system, archives every chat, code and game session as a .pfm file; there are 200 chat archives in his local chats folder and about 5,000 .pfm files in total across that data directory.", "Other projects reference it: Glasshouse (a join over the .pfm chat archive), the Werbos importers and exporters, and an architecture note for the Alongside app that uses PFM as the export layer, not the database.", "The repo's own capture and recall skills are used to save insights as .pfm during Claude Code sessions."], "links": [{"label": "Viewer and converter", "url": "https://getpfm.io/"}, {"label": "npm", "url": "https://www.npmjs.com/package/get-pfm"}, {"label": "PyPI", "url": "https://pypi.org/project/get-pfm/"}], "buildLogTitle": "test runs, Nov 2026", "buildLog": "$ pytest tests\n........................................................................ [ 42%]\n........................................................................ [ 85%]\n.........................                                                [100%]\n169 passed in 1.93s\n\n$ cd pfm-js && node --test dist/esm/__tests__/index.test.js dist/esm/__tests__/conformance.test.js\n# tests 55\n# pass 55\n# fail 0\n\n$ git log --format='%h %ad %s' --date=short\n5702d28 2026-06-07 docs(readme): refresh stale content + fix version skew\ne99c692 2026-04-29 design: tiered archival for conversations (hot/warm/cold via pfm merge)\nd70c08e 2026-02-25 Add SECURITY.md with 3 open challenges\nf49995d 2026-02-22 Add GitHub Actions workflow for npm publish with provenance\n83a41c0 2026-02-21 Bump to 0.2.1 - security hardening for merge/geminio\nc7c9e31 2026-02-21 Add pfm merge/geminio + Claude Code capture/recall skills\n6e4e54a 2026-02-20 Bump to 0.2.0 - add pfm export command for fine-tuning data\nb80b80f 2026-02-20 Bump to 0.1.9 - fix stdin pipe crash in create command", "slug": "pfm", "n": 13, "platforms": "npm · PyPI · Web", "category": "Applied AI · Infrastructure", "shots": [{"src": "img/pfm-00.jpg", "caption": "getpfm.io: view and convert .pfm files in the browser; nothing is uploaded", "w": 1400, "h": 620}]}, {"name": "Block Crew", "tagline": "AI personas that really play Minecraft, 24/7", "oneLiner": "Block Crew puts a small crew of AI personas on an always-on Minecraft server where they chop, mine, build, farm, fight and die, using a local 3B model only for choices and chat.", "problem": "LLM-driven game agents tend to hallucinate what they did and treat model output as commands, which makes them unreliable and unsafe to give control. Block Crew asks how much an agent can do if the model is kept off the critical path.", "whatItIs": "A Node.js system of eight mineflayer bots, each a persona from Jason's separate persona project, living on a self-hosted vanilla Minecraft server. They run on their own schedule, from sheep and bed to shelter, food, camp, farm and armor, and take plain-English orders from players in chat.", "howItWorks": ["Three layers, in order of who wins: reflexes in code (eat, fight, flee creepers, avoid lava) at about ten ticks a second; jobs where code does every swing and step; and a 3B model that only picks what to do next when nobody has asked, and talks.", "Chat orders are parsed by rules in src/commands.js, never by the model, so 'stop' always means stop. Orders chain with 'then' (build a cabin then chop 10 logs).", "The model is llama3.2:3b via Ollama, queried one question at a time, and is told what actually happened from the bots' own logs, so it narrates real events.", "A progress tree drives autonomy by game day: shelter, stone tools, furnace and torches, farm and breeding, leather armor, then garden wall and watchtower.", "A recipe book of buildings is written against roles (wall, post, roof) not block names, so any palette fits any recipe. Layout code places towns (hamlet, walled town, river village) with roads first and doors facing roads, plus a 'lived-in' dressing pass.", "Runs in Docker on a home server bound to a private Tailscale address; vanilla server chosen over Paper after measuring position corrections that made bots barely move."], "aiAngle": ["Deliberate separation of concerns: deterministic reflexes and rule-parsed commands for anything safety or control related, a small model only for open-ended choice and conversation.", "Prompt-injection guard: model output has leading slashes stripped because the bot is a server operator and nothing the model says may become a command.", "Truthful narration: the model is given real logged events, and 'what are you doing' is answered from state, not generated guesses.", "Local inference on a 3B model with one-at-a-time queuing sized to the home server.", "Verification by behavior: 71 test scenes drive real bots in a real world over RCON, plus a walk test that counts server corrections on real terrain.", "Recipes are validated against the game's own data and fail on overlaps or unreachable doors."], "stack": ["Node.js", "mineflayer", "mineflayer-pathfinder", "minecraft-data", "Ollama (llama3.2:3b)", "Docker", "RCON", "Python (preview tools)"], "metrics": [{"label": "Commits", "value": "148"}, {"label": "Commits in last 2 days", "value": "26"}, {"label": "Source LOC", "value": "~8,600"}, {"label": "Test scenes", "value": "71 files"}, {"label": "Personas", "value": "8"}, {"label": "Town layouts", "value": "5"}], "status": "In active development", "timeline": "Nov 6 – Nov 11, 2026 (ongoing)", "progress": ["Reflexes, jobs and 3B brain layers working end to end.", "Rule-based command grammar covering gather, mine, build, farm, fetch, breed, carry and more.", "Building recipes from mud hut to saloon and modern house, plus town layouts and micro-prop kits.", "A field guide page with the progress tree as a clickable node map and every phrase the bots understand.", "Latest: new-world handling, travel beds, iron-finding and health-regen fixes. Next: let them build the stair-based recipes in survival."], "dogfooding": ["Jason plays on the server himself; commit messages record fixes for what he saw and showed the bots.", "Bot logs under data/ let a returning player hear what really happened while away.", "Fixes land daily from live observation, for example a bot stuck at 5 health because it stopped eating at 17 food."], "links": [], "buildLogTitle": "recent commits, Nov 2026", "buildLog": "$ git log --format='%h %ad %s' --date=short   # block-crew, last 3 days\n2c66213 2026-11-11 Hurt, he eats up to 18 food: health only regenerates from 18, and at 17 he sat at 5 health for good\nbbb2694 2026-11-11 Unwedge: pressed flush into a step, the client slips into the block, the server puts him back\nbb70016 2026-11-11 Field guide: how Werbos thinks, the progress tree as a clickable node map, and every phrase he understands\n4ed709b 2026-11-11 Dusk on a trip: far from home with no bed within 64 blocks and one in his pack, he sleeps where he stands\n88d4550 2026-11-11 A new seed is a new world: he keeps what he did, his counts and his tastes, and forgets home, chests,\n7a06a44 2026-11-10 Needing iron, try every spot he knows nearest first (6 at most, whole veins)\n6147341 2026-11-10 Gather wood and stone until the tool wears out (a stack at most), extra to the chest; quarry retries\n2809177 2026-11-10 No digging out of reach (the server ignored it and the ore came back)\nacea477 2026-11-10 After digging iron, wait for the drop to land and look 16 blocks round for it: cliff ore drops fall\n39278ed 2026-11-10 Say why an iron ore gave no raw iron\n\n$ git rev-list --count HEAD\n148\n$ git log --since=2026-11-10 --oneline | wc -l\n26\n$ ls test | wc -l            # real-bot scene tests run against a vanilla 1.21 server over RCON\n71\n$ git ls-files src | xargs cat | wc -l\n8605", "slug": "block-crew", "n": 14, "platforms": "Server · Minecraft", "category": "Applied AI · Agents", "shots": [{"src": "img/block-crew-00.jpg", "caption": "Building recipe ladder: mud hut to saloon and modern house", "w": 1400, "h": 422}, {"src": "img/block-crew-01.jpg", "caption": "Town layouts planned and checked for walkable doors", "w": 1400, "h": 863}, {"src": "img/block-crew-02.jpg", "caption": "Micro-build prop kit", "w": 1400, "h": 322}]}, {"name": "MRT Brain", "tagline": "Offline, cited answers over mandated-reporter law", "oneLiner": "A browser-only assistant that answers questions from 35 Mandated Reporter Training documents and declines when nothing matches.", "problem": "Mandated-reporter law differs by state and changes by year, and an ungrounded chatbot can state the wrong rule with confidence. The training provider needed answers that cite their own course content and say \"I don't know\" instead of guessing.", "whatItIs": "A static page with a precomputed corpus of 2,342 fact chunks built from the company's course documents. The user picks a state, asks a question, and gets the closest passage with its source file and a verdict. Nothing leaves the device and there is no LLM. It sits beside the live Mandated Reporter Training LMS, which serves 5,000+ organizations (that figure is from Jason's own description, not from this repo).", "howItWorks": ["build_corpus.py (417 lines) extracts text from PDF, DOCX, PPTX and XLSX sources (Office formats parsed with the stdlib), chunks them into facts and emits web/corpus.js, so the page works from file://", "Retrieval is TF-IDF cosine similarity built in the browser on load; corpus is 12,806 words of vocabulary", "A deterministic router first picks the state's tree (for California, 1,108 state leaves plus 732 shared leaves) and ranks inside it with tree-local IDF, state law before national material", "An 'as of year' filter drops chunks newer than the chosen vintage", "Every answer carries a model-free verdict: GROUNDED, UNSCOPED, WEAK or ABSTAIN, plus the source file and 'also see' sources", "Corpus and Sources views expose everything the assistant knows and where each fact came from"], "aiAngle": ["Retrieval with abstention: a tunable score threshold makes the system decline instead of answering weakly, the central guardrail for a legal-adjacent domain", "Provenance on every answer: source document path, state and vintage, so claims can be checked against the course", "Deterministic routing and filtering outside the ranker: jurisdiction is a hard constraint, not a similarity hint", "No generation means no hallucination; the tradeoff is that answers are passages, not synthesis (the README lists boosting filename/heading matches as the next improvement)", "Honest failure handling: one image-only PDF extracted empty and is flagged as needing OCR; the UI states 'It can be wrong'"], "stack": ["Python", "JavaScript", "TF-IDF retrieval", "HTML/CSS", "Static hosting (Netlify)"], "metrics": [{"label": "Source documents", "value": "35"}, {"label": "Cited fact chunks", "value": "2,342"}, {"label": "Vocabulary", "value": "12,806 words"}, {"label": "California chunks", "value": "1,108 state + 732 shared"}, {"label": "LMS orgs (Jason's figure)", "value": "5,000+"}], "status": "Working prototype; LMS it supports is live", "timeline": "Built July 2026 (file dates); no git history", "progress": ["Corpus builder and 3-view app (Ask, Corpus, Sources) working offline", "State routing, vintage filter and verdict labels added", "Next: filename and heading boosting, OCR for the scanned PDF, embed as a widget in the LMS"], "dogfooding": ["Built over the real course content of Jason's own training company", "Designed to embed in the live LMS as an iframe or widget"], "links": [{"label": "Mandated Reporter Training (LMS)", "url": "https://mandatedreportertraining.com/"}], "buildLogTitle": "corpus build and a sample query", "buildLog": "$ python3 build_corpus.py\n# sources -> chunks -> web/corpus.js\nfiles indexed ........ 35\nfact chunks .......... 2,342\nvocabulary ........... 12,806 words\nstate tree: California 1,108 state + 732 shared leaves\nscanned PDF (AB 2085) extracted empty, flagged for OCR\n\n# Ask view, state = California, \"Who is a mandated reporter?\"\nverdict: GROUNDED\nrouted to California tree, 1,108 state + 732 shared leaves\nsource: Course Content Outlines/Child Abuse/California/\n        California Volunteers Training Course Content (.docx)\nalso see: California Human Resources, California Child Care Providers\n# verdicts available: GROUNDED / UNSCOPED / WEAK / ABSTAIN\n# no LLM, no network; index builds in the browser on load", "slug": "mrt-assistant", "n": 15, "platforms": "Web (offline)", "category": "Applied AI · Retrieval", "shots": [{"src": "img/mrt-assistant-00.jpg", "caption": "Ask view, California, with verdict and source", "w": 1200, "h": 1100}, {"src": "img/mrt-assistant-01.jpg", "caption": "Opening state", "w": 1200, "h": 900}, {"src": "img/mrt-assistant-02.jpg", "caption": "MRT for Individuals", "w": 781, "h": 526}, {"src": "img/mrt-assistant-03.jpg", "caption": "MRT for Organizations", "w": 781, "h": 525}, {"src": "img/mrt-assistant-04.jpg", "caption": "certficate qr example", "w": 1112, "h": 770}, {"src": "img/mrt-assistant-05.jpg", "caption": "labtop with a screenshot of the course", "w": 1400, "h": 826}]}, {"name": "CryptoBlocks", "tagline": "Kids snap blocks, then read the real code", "oneLiner": "A visual coding platform for kids and beginners where every block is real JavaScript and Python underneath, on the web and as a desktop app.", "problem": "Block coders like Scratch remove the syntax barrier but are a dead end: nothing you build teaches you the code behind it, so kids quit at the gap between toy and real programming. CryptoBlocks is built to be the bridge.", "whatItIs": "A Blockly-based editor with 313 built-in block definitions across 21 category files, where every block compiles to both JavaScript and Python. Kids can peek at the generated code, write their own function and turn it into a block, collaborate live, and share projects. Packaged for the web and as a Tauri desktop app, with a Hugo marketing site and blog.", "howItWorks": ["Editor: React 19, TypeScript, Vite and Google Blockly (Zelos renderer). Each block is a data record with JS and Python implementations plus unit-test cases, so Create Block can validate a kid's function before it becomes a block.", "Execution is sandboxed in the browser: JavaScript in a locked-down iframe, Python through Pyodide (CPython compiled to WebAssembly). Source-map markers link code lines back to blocks for bidirectional debugging.", "A safety layer (src/safety) injects wrapped fetch, WebSocket and other APIs into the sandbox with a domain blocklist and SafeSearch enforcement, since the users are children.", "Real-time collaboration uses Yjs CRDTs over PartyKit WebSockets with six-letter room codes; projects live in Turso through Netlify Functions (Shareplace: upload, like, remix).", "Client-side ML blocks: MobileNet image classification, MediaPipe hand tracking, speech in and out, plus micro:bit control over WebBluetooth.", "Desktop build via Tauri v2 (Rust); Playwright end-to-end tests and Vitest unit tests."], "aiAngle": ["brain/: a char-level GPT trained from random weights (6 layers, 6 heads, 240-dim, 4.24M parameters) on a 3.15 MB synthetic corpus about CryptoBlocks only. No base model, so the 'only answers about CryptoBlocks' constraint is architectural, not a prompt. Best validation loss 0.067 after about 35 minutes on Apple MPS.", "The corpus is generated combinatorially from a facts file (every fact asked many ways, answered many ways), with off-topic questions trained to deflect. scope_test.py asserts on-topic vs off-topic behavior.", "The model was exported to run in the browser (export_web.py, in-browser worker), so the site's chat widget does client-side inference with no hosted API.", "VibeBlocks: plain-words-to-blocks behind a swappable Composer interface. The real engine is Jason's model-free Werbos composer, which returns a brick tree or an honest abstain; a stub stands in for tests. The UI shows which engine answered.", "Client-side educational ML primitives (classifier, Markov chain, regression, sentiment) are exposed as blocks so kids build with models rather than call them."], "stack": ["TypeScript", "React 19", "Vite", "Blockly", "Monaco", "Pyodide", "Yjs + PartyKit", "Tauri v2 (Rust)", "Turso", "Netlify", "Hugo", "PyTorch", "Vitest + Playwright"], "metrics": [{"label": "Commits", "value": "742"}, {"label": "Built-in blocks", "value": "313"}, {"label": "Challenges", "value": "141 in 27 packs"}, {"label": "TS/Rust source lines (app)", "value": "65.7k"}, {"label": "Test cases declared", "value": "731+"}, {"label": "From-scratch model", "value": "4.24M params"}], "status": "Live, in active development (v0.5)", "timeline": "Feb 2026 – Nov 2026", "progress": ["Feb-Apr 2026: 716 of 742 commits; core editor, challenges, Shareplace, collab, Scratch .sb3 import, PWA export, Tauri desktop app.", "v0.5 (Apr 2026): workspace tools, unified scope system, bidirectional code-to-block debugging, full security sweep.", "Jun-Jul 2026: from-scratch CryptoBlocks brain trained and moved to in-browser inference.", "Autumn 2026: VibeBlocks composer, My Blocks Library, ZTA analytics, Gatekeep sign-in, automated dependency PRs.", "Next: bidirectional code and block editing, classroom licenses."], "dogfooding": ["Jason built his own toolchain into it: ZTA analytics (CSP allows it), Gatekeep sign-in replaced Clerk in the git history, and Mycelium opened the dependency upgrade PRs.", "VibeBlocks calls his Werbos composer directly, so Werbos is a live dependency, not a demo.", "The training pipeline reuses his werbos Python environment."], "links": [{"label": "Live site", "url": "https://getcryptoblocks.com/"}], "buildLogTitle": "brain training log and recent commits", "buildLog": "$ cat brain/train.log      # from-scratch model that only knows CryptoBlocks\ndevice=mps  params=4.24M  vocab=69  corpus=3.15MB  tokens=2.99M  turn-aligned starts=22948\nstep     0 | train 4.205 | val 4.204 | lr 2.4e-06 | 19s\nstep   250 | train 1.724 | val 1.724 | lr 3.0e-04 | 231s\nstep   500 | train 0.387 | val 0.392 | lr 2.8e-04 | 433s\nstep   750 | train 0.130 | val 0.130 | lr 2.6e-04 | 634s\nstep  1750 | train 0.068 | val 0.068 | lr 9.1e-05 | 1462s\nstep  2000 | train 0.067 | val 0.068 | lr 5.8e-05 | 1673s\nstep  2500 | train 0.067 | val 0.067 | lr 3.0e-05 | 2089s\ndone. best val loss 0.067. checkpoint in ckpt/model.pt\n\n$ git log --oneline | head -7\n8d92b0e Merge pull request #7 from <owner>/mycelium/fix-parent-202611030947-plist\n8a34cb0 mycelium: bump plist to clear quick-xml advisories\n691e761 mycelium: upgrade 36 vulnerable package(s)\nf6a04d8 app: rename My First Vibe App mode to VibeBlocks\n57dd34f app: My First Vibe App — plain words → blocks via a swappable composer\n36ccf3e app: My Blocks Library — browse, edit, delete, and move custom blocks\n0615765 app CSP: allow Zero Trust Analytics, drop Clerk", "slug": "cryptoblocks", "n": 16, "platforms": "Web · Mac · PC", "category": "Applied AI · Education", "shots": [{"src": "img/cryptoblocks-00.jpg", "caption": "The block editor: 25 block categories, from Basics to AI, Crypto, micro:bit and Vision", "w": 1400, "h": 674}, {"src": "img/cryptoblocks-01.jpg", "caption": "Peek Code: the same program as real JavaScript, Python or HTML", "w": 1400, "h": 674}, {"src": "img/cryptoblocks-02.jpg", "caption": "Example projects, beginner to intermediate", "w": 1400, "h": 674}, {"src": "img/cryptoblocks-03.jpg", "caption": "Challenge Hub: themed packs, 426 stars to earn", "w": 1400, "h": 674}, {"src": "img/cryptoblocks-04.jpg", "caption": "getcryptoblocks.com: blocks beside generated JS", "w": 1400, "h": 875}, {"src": "img/cryptoblocks-05.jpg", "caption": "The editor (local production build)", "w": 1400, "h": 875}, {"src": "img/cryptoblocks-06.jpg", "caption": "About CryptoBlocks — Our Story", "w": 1400, "h": 875}, {"src": "img/cryptoblocks-07.jpg", "caption": "Build", "w": 1400, "h": 875}, {"src": "img/cryptoblocks-08.jpg", "caption": "Learn to Code with CryptoBlocks", "w": 1400, "h": 875}, {"src": "img/cryptoblocks-09.jpg", "caption": "Security Reviews", "w": 1400, "h": 875}]}, {"name": "Werbos.design", "tagline": "Deterministic site builder, no model in the loop", "oneLiner": "A website builder that composes finished, accessible sites from a short brief using predefined components, layouts and variants, with no AI in the generate path.", "problem": "AI site generators pay frontier-model tokens on every build, give different output each time, and can hand back broken markup. For a small business site you want something predictable, cheap to host, and checkable.", "whatItIs": "A browser app (React, TypeScript) over a pure TypeScript engine. A one-paragraph brief becomes a content model, then a page assembled from a registry of 39 section types with 298 designs, styled by persona, skeleton, rhythm, figure and mark. Output is self-contained framework-free HTML. The full site state lives in the URL hash, so the link is the site. Jason's own portfolio site was generated with it.", "howItWorks": ["brief -> buildContent() -> SECTIONS[k].render(content) -> one HTML file. Copy comes from hash-seeded pattern libraries (product and trade registers), so the same brief and style give byte-identical output.", "Variety comes from independent axes: 12 personas x 7 skeletons x 4 rhythms x 9 hero figures x 13 brand marks, counted from the engine registries. That is 39,312 visual combinations before accent (10), vibe (3) or any choice of sections.", "Every page is an ordered list of block instances drawn from 39 section types and 298 designs, so the number of distinct pages is far larger than the style count; no one has enumerated it.", "State-as-document: {brief, style, pages, ops} is one codec used for both the URL hash and SQLite. Visual edits are recorded as ops keyed by structural DOM paths and replayed in a fixed order.", "A built-in audit (health.ts) checks one h1, heading order, link targets, WCAG contrast and SEO on composed HTML; a sweep script renders every design in every persona and every page type across skeletons and rhythms.", "Optional server (Bun and SQLite): Gatekeep passkey SSO, saved projects, versions, shares, publish, and a billing model of build free, pay to take the site out."], "aiAngle": ["Honest framing: Werbos.design is deliberately non-AI. The generate path has no LLM, which is a design choice for determinism, zero per-build cost, and output you can test.", "Where a model is allowed, it sits behind a hard boundary: it fills a small validated JSON brief or emits actions from a fixed verb list (server/brief.ts, server/edit.ts). It never writes HTML or state.", "Measured, not assumed: Jason tested llama3.2:3b and llama3.1:8b locally. Both returned valid JSON, and both misread 'local-first AI' as 'local community AI'. So literal extraction from the user's words wins over the model, and the model only fills gaps.", "Every brief field carries provenance (said, model, default) shown in the UI, and an unknown style falls back to house style and says so. Confident invention is treated as the failure mode.", "With no model reachable, the edit box falls back to a deterministic parser, so the app works offline.", "Tests are checked by mutation: HANDOFF.md lists the code mutations the suite catches, on the principle that a suite that cannot fail is decoration."], "stack": ["TypeScript", "React", "Vite", "Bun", "SQLite", "Hugo", "Tailwind CSS", "Netlify", "Gatekeep OIDC", "Ollama (optional)"], "metrics": [{"label": "Visual style combinations", "value": "39,312"}, {"label": "Section types / designs", "value": "39 / 298"}, {"label": "Sweep renders (run today)", "value": "3,576 sections, 2,352 pages"}, {"label": "Commits", "value": "123"}, {"label": "Test cases declared", "value": "491"}, {"label": "LLM calls in generate path", "value": "0"}], "status": "Live, in active development", "timeline": "Sep 2026 – Nov 2026", "progress": ["Sep 2026: 113 of 123 commits; engine, builder, visual editor, state codec, deploy targets, health audit.", "Multi-page sites, local-business and trade copy registers, editor with undo, templates and import.", "Oct 2026: person register, projects, spotlight and colophon sections; $5 for 50 exports billing.", "Nov 2026: opt-in search listing with JSON-LD, ZTA visit counting, dependency upgrades.", "Next per HANDOFF.md: real publishing (custom domains, form submissions that land somewhere)."], "dogfooding": ["Jason's portfolio at jasonsutter87.github.io was generated with it and carries a 'Made with werbos.design' badge linking back to the builder.", "It runs on his own stack: Gatekeep for sign-in, ZTA for visit counting (commit ac5a18b), Mycelium for dependency PRs.", "The sibling Werbos repos supply the CLI composer; werbos.design is the public client-side surface of the same deterministic idea."], "links": [{"label": "Live builder", "url": "https://werbos-design.netlify.app/"}, {"label": "Site built with it", "url": "https://jasonsutter87.github.io/"}], "buildLogTitle": "sweep run and recent commits, 11 Nov 2026", "buildLog": "$ cd app && bun run scripts/sweep.ts      # every design x persona, every page type, audited\nsection renders: 3576\npage renders:    2352\ndeterministic:   true\n\nclean\n\n$ git log --format='%h %ad %s' --date=short | head -7\n469e8fe 2026-11-02 Merge pull request #2 from <owner>/mycelium/fix-202611030628\n08def1a 2026-11-02 mycelium: upgrade 17 vulnerable package(s)\nd441e86 2026-11-02 feat: opt-in search listing: title, description, canonical, JSON-LD\nac5a18b 2026-10-31 Add Zero Trust Analytics visit counting\nfe7d614 2026-10-26 fix(builder): a page with a colophon keeps its way back in the designer\n51e4139 2026-10-26 feat: spotlight section: two or three things, each with figures and links\nf8f9005 2026-10-26 feat: colophon section: the brief a page was built from, printed beside it", "slug": "werbos-design", "n": 17, "platforms": "Web", "category": "Product", "shots": [{"src": "img/werbos-design-00.jpg", "caption": "The designer: brief on the left, a finished site on the right, no LLM, about a millisecond per build", "w": 1400, "h": 675}, {"src": "img/werbos-design-01.jpg", "caption": "Shuffle: eight complete sites from one brief in about 4 ms, for $0", "w": 1400, "h": 675}, {"src": "img/werbos-design-02.jpg", "caption": "Start: describe the site in one sentence or pick one of eight starting points", "w": 1400, "h": 675}, {"src": "img/werbos-design-03.jpg", "caption": "werbos.design: brief in, eight starting sites", "w": 1400, "h": 875}, {"src": "img/werbos-design-04.jpg", "caption": "jasonsutter87.github.io, generated by werbos.design", "w": 1400, "h": 875}]}, {"name": "Gatekeep", "tagline": "Hosted passkey sign-in over standard OpenID Connect", "oneLiner": "Passwordless authentication for apps: users sign in with passkeys, Google or GitHub and the app receives a signed OpenID Connect token, with no SDK required.", "problem": "Passwords mean resets and leaks, and per-user auth vendors lock apps into proprietary SDKs. Small teams want phishing-resistant login that any OIDC library can consume.", "whatItIs": "Gatekeep started as a from-scratch WebAuthn plus OIDC server with a written protocol spec, then became a hosted multi-tenant product with a developer dashboard, Stripe billing by monthly active users, webhooks, custom domains and branding. Free up to 5,000 monthly active users. Jason's other products use it for login.", "howItWorks": ["Bun + SQLite reference server (9-table schema, about 30 MB idle) with 13 wire endpoints: 5 OIDC and 8 for WebAuthn, enrollment and recovery.", "Handrolled Ed25519 JWT signing with key rotation, JWKS publishing and a 35-day grace window; PKCE S256 mandatory; every opaque token stored hashed.", "WebAuthn verification with sign-count regression detection and credential auto-revocation; refresh-token family revocation on replay; recovery signs out everywhere.", "Multi-tenancy: control plane, host-based routing, per-environment issuers and keys, nothing shared between tenants.", "Google and GitHub sign-in added as another way to prove identity at the gate page, while apps still get an EdDSA token whose sub is a Gatekeep ULID.", "Stripe Billing Meters bill by monthly active users; auth event log with plan-based retention, webhooks and an admin API on higher tiers."], "aiAngle": ["No LLM features; identity systems should be deterministic and auditable.", "Built with Claude Code agents against a 4,000+ line written protocol spec with byte-pinned test vectors (ULID, SHA-256, PKCE, Ed25519 JWT, WebAuthn assertion, sign-count matrix).", "Spec-first, vector-tested design is the approach I bring to agent work: a precise contract an agent's output can be checked against.", "The repo README records a 99/100 archaeology review of the protocol spec (long-term readability)."], "stack": ["Bun", "TypeScript", "SQLite", "WebAuthn", "OpenID Connect", "Ed25519", "Stripe", "Playwright", "Hugo", "Cloudflare", "Netlify"], "metrics": [{"label": "Commits", "value": "67"}, {"label": "Tests passing", "value": "187"}, {"label": "Source lines", "value": "7,400+"}, {"label": "Test lines (unit + e2e)", "value": "4,400+"}, {"label": "Wire endpoints", "value": "13"}, {"label": "Free tier", "value": "5,000 MAU"}], "status": "Live, open for signups", "timeline": "May 2026 - Nov 2026", "progress": ["May 2026: protocol v2, reference server and production hardening (Caddy, key store).", "Oct 29: multi-tenancy, developer dashboard, Stripe MAU billing, webhooks, custom domains.", "Oct 30: Google and GitHub sign-in, simple pricing ($25 / $99 a month with 10,000 users, then $0.02 each), Playwright e2e suite.", "Nov 11: account page to add and remove passkeys.", "Next: a second independent implementation of the spec."], "dogfooding": ["Glyphwork uses Gatekeep for its customer sign-in and dashboard.", "The developer dashboard signs in with Gatekeep itself.", "Can run self-hosted on his own box via Cloudflare Tunnel with a one-command setup script."], "links": [{"label": "Live site", "url": "https://gatekeepauth.com/"}], "buildLogTitle": "bun test, Nov 2026", "buildLog": "$ bun test\ntest/billing.test.ts:\ntest/dashboard.test.ts:\ntest/server-ratelimit.test.ts:\n[enroll/start] per-email rate limit hit (retry-after=60s); silently swallowing per 8.6\ntest/server.test.ts:\n[keys] no active signing key; minting one (dev/first-run path)\ntest/tenancy.test.ts:\n\n 187 pass\n 0 fail\n 877 expect() calls\nRan 187 tests across 20 files. [28.43s]\n\n$ git log --format='%ad %s' --date=short\n2026-11-11 Account page: see, add and remove passkeys, and sign out\n2026-10-30 Playwright end-to-end suite for the hosted product\n2026-10-30 Continue with Google / GitHub on the dashboard sign-in page\n2026-10-29 Stripe billing by monthly active users (Billing Meters)\n2026-10-29 Multi-tenancy: control plane, host routing, per-environment issuers\n2026-10-29 Developer dashboard, signed in with gatekeep itself", "slug": "gatekeep", "n": 18, "platforms": "Web", "category": "B2B", "shots": [{"src": "img/gatekeep-00.jpg", "caption": "Marketing site with sample signed id_token", "w": 1400, "h": 875}, {"src": "img/gatekeep-01.jpg", "caption": "Create your account: an email field and a Send signup link button, with a back link", "w": 1080, "h": 720}, {"src": "img/gatekeep-02.jpg", "caption": "Check your inbox: a message saying a link has been sent, and to check spam if it doesn't arrive within a minute", "w": 1200, "h": 840}, {"src": "img/gatekeep-03.jpg", "caption": "Lost your passkey? Enter your email for a recovery link. Completing recovery revokes all existing passkeys on your account.", "w": 1080, "h": 720}, {"src": "img/gatekeep-04.jpg", "caption": "The Gatekeep sign-in page for an app called Acme Notes, with Sign in with passkey, Create an account and Lost your passkey? buttons", "w": 1080, "h": 720}, {"src": "img/gatekeep-05.jpg", "caption": "Email-first sign-in: enter your address and Gatekeep shows only the passkeys registered to it, with a Continue button and a back link", "w": 1080, "h": 720}, {"src": "img/gatekeep-06.jpg", "caption": "Gatekeep Docs", "w": 1400, "h": 875}, {"src": "img/gatekeep-07.jpg", "caption": "Pricing", "w": 1400, "h": 875}, {"src": "img/gatekeep-08.jpg", "caption": "Admin API", "w": 1400, "h": 875}, {"src": "img/gatekeep-09.jpg", "caption": "Branding and email sender", "w": 1400, "h": 875}, {"src": "img/gatekeep-10.jpg", "caption": "Creating an account", "w": 1400, "h": 875}, {"src": "img/gatekeep-11.jpg", "caption": "Custom domains", "w": 1400, "h": 875}]}, {"name": "Zero Trust Analytics", "tagline": "Website analytics with no cookies and no PII", "oneLiner": "Privacy-first web analytics for site owners who want visitors, pages, countries and events without storing IPs or putting a consent banner in front of readers.", "problem": "Mainstream analytics tools track people across the web, need cookie banners, and keep personal data the site owner then has to defend. Most small sites only want to know how many people came, from where, and to which page.", "whatItIs": "A hosted analytics SaaS at ztas.io: a small embeddable script, a dashboard (visitors, time on site, country, OS, entry and exit pages, custom events, chat-widget reporting), a stats API with API keys, and Stripe billing priced on monthly events with unlimited sites on every plan. It can also be self-hosted with Docker. Sign-in is passkey-only through Jason's own SSO product, Gatekeep.", "howItWorks": ["A 2.4 KB tracker (per the live site; source in src/analytics.js and analytics.core.js) stores nothing on the visitor's device and sends pageviews and custom events to a tracking endpoint.", "Visitor and session identity is derived server-side from hashed inputs with a daily-rotating salt, so raw IPs are never stored and IDs cannot be joined across days.", "Serverless backend: 54 function files under netlify/functions (track, stats, realtime, events, funnels, goals, heatmaps, export, import-ga, webhooks, teams, usage, Stripe checkout/portal/webhook) over a Turso (libSQL) database.", "Dashboard is a Next.js and TypeScript app (app/) in front of the API; the marketing, docs and pricing site is Hugo on Netlify.", "Authentication moved to Gatekeep (passkeys); programs use API keys as Bearer tokens against the Stats API. An auth-mode switch (oidc, password, none) supports self-hosting.", "Plan limits are enforced in code (plan-limits, usage, usage-notification), including retention windows and a customEvents feature flag that the events endpoint checks."], "aiAngle": ["Honest framing: the analytics core is deliberately not AI. Deterministic hashing and aggregation are what make a privacy claim auditable.", "The relevant AI engineering is on the guardrail side: a privacy-by-construction data model (no PII is collected, so no model or vendor downstream can leak it).", "The dashboard reports chat-widget activity next to pageviews, so it measures AI chat products without logging the conversations.", "Security work was agent-assisted and tracked: an automated white-box pentest (BLACK-TEAM-PENTEST.md, 2026-02-09) produced 42 findings (5 critical, 12 high, 17 medium, 8 low), triaged in VULNERABILITY-TRACKER.md with fix status per ID.", "Dependency patching is automated with Mycelium: 6 merged mycelium/* upgrade PRs in the git history, each opened headless and reviewed before merge."], "stack": ["JavaScript", "TypeScript", "Next.js", "Netlify Functions", "Turso (libSQL)", "Hugo", "Tailwind CSS", "Stripe", "Gatekeep passkeys / OIDC", "Jest", "Vitest", "Docker"], "metrics": [{"label": "Commits", "value": "409"}, {"label": "Jest tests passing", "value": "585 (34 suites)"}, {"label": "Serverless function files", "value": "54"}, {"label": "Pentest findings triaged", "value": "42"}, {"label": "Paid plan tiers", "value": "5 + Free"}, {"label": "Tracker size (live site)", "value": "2.4 KB"}], "status": "Live, in active development", "timeline": "May 2025 – Nov 2026", "progress": ["Core tracking, stats API, dashboard and Stripe billing shipped (heavy build burst Dec 2025 – Jan 2026: 354 of 409 commits).", "Black-team security audit run in Feb 2026; critical SQL injection, default-secret and CSRF findings marked fixed in the tracker, some items still open.", "Nov 2026: custom-events report and chat-widget reporting added to the dashboard; passkey sign-in and sign-out through Gatekeep.", "Pricing page live with Free, Starter $9, Growth $19, Business $49, Scale $99 and Enterprise tiers.", "Next: finish open pentest items and get the public demo back up once the site has traffic (demo is hidden for now)."], "dogfooding": ["Jason's own sites report to ZTA: CryptoBlocks and werbos.design have commits that add the ZTA script and allow it in their CSP.", "The sign-in layer is Gatekeep, another of his products; recent commits wire ZTA's sign-out and Manage passkeys flow into it.", "Dependency upgrades come from Mycelium PRs against this repo."], "links": [{"label": "Live site", "url": "https://ztas.io/"}, {"label": "Pricing", "url": "https://ztas.io/pricing/"}, {"label": "Docs", "url": "https://ztas.io/docs/"}], "buildLogTitle": "test run and recent commits, 11 Nov 2026", "buildLog": "$ npm test   (zero-trust-analytics, Jest)\n...\nPASS tests/__tests__/events.test.js\n  /api/events\n    ✓ requires a signed-in user (5 ms)\n    ✓ refuses a site the user does not own (2 ms)\n    ✓ respects the customEvents plan feature (2 ms)\n    ✓ returns events for the period (2 ms)\n    ✓ caps long ranges at the default instead of scanning a year (3 ms)\n    ✓ only accepts known name prefixes (40 ms)\n    ✓ serves repeat views from the cache (1 ms)\n    ✓ only allows GET\n\nTest Suites: 34 passed, 34 total\nTests:       585 passed, 585 total\nTime:        10.838 s\n\n$ git log --oneline | head -6\nb84512a Merge pull request #13 from zerotrustanalytics/fix/visible-sign-out\n6a8e6e4 Make Sign out findable, and return to the app after it\n80f8d02 Merge pull request #12 from zerotrustanalytics/fix/gatekeep-account-signout\n5651ae1 Sign out of gatekeep too, and link Manage passkeys to the account page\n6398c67 Merge pull request #11 from zerotrustanalytics/feat/custom-events-report\nd79f189 feat: report custom events and chat widgets in the dashboard\n4aa51ea mycelium: upgrade 4 vulnerable package(s)", "slug": "zta", "n": 19, "platforms": "Web", "category": "B2B", "shots": [{"src": "img/zta-00.jpg", "caption": "ztas.io home page", "w": 1400, "h": 875}, {"src": "img/zta-01.jpg", "caption": "Event-based pricing, unlimited sites", "w": 1400, "h": 875}, {"src": "img/zta-02.jpg", "caption": "Docs: custom events, chat widgets, privacy model", "w": 1400, "h": 875}, {"src": "img/zta-03.jpg", "caption": "API Reference", "w": 1400, "h": 875}, {"src": "img/zta-04.jpg", "caption": "Chat Widgets", "w": 1400, "h": 875}, {"src": "img/zta-05.jpg", "caption": "Custom Events", "w": 1400, "h": 875}, {"src": "img/zta-06.jpg", "caption": "Data Collected", "w": 1400, "h": 875}]}, {"name": "CookieAgent", "tagline": "Self-hosted cookie consent that actually blocks trackers", "oneLiner": "A lightweight consent script and drag-and-drop banner builder that keeps trackers blocked until a visitor opts in, for sites that need EU privacy compliance.", "problem": "Consent platforms like Cookiebot charge per domain, and some only hide the banner while trackers still fire. Compliance needs trackers to stay off until consent and a record of each decision.", "whatItIs": "CookieAgent (repo name cookietracker) is a one-line script tag. Customers mark tracker scripts as inert, build a banner in a React builder, and the embed blocks cookies until the visitor accepts. Each decision is logged to an API as a self-contained audit record. It has a live builder and a demo customer site.", "howItWorks": ["Trackers are tagged type=\"text/plain\" data-cookieconsent=\"statistics\", which browsers never execute. The blocker re-creates them in order after consent, so external scripts load before dependent inline code.", "ct.js is vanilla TypeScript bundled with esbuild (about 16 kb, zero dependencies). It renders the banner inside a Shadow DOM with focus trap, Esc handling and three layouts.", "Consent is stored in localStorage with a cookie mirror (Safari ITP safe), parsed fail-closed, with separate banner-version and cookie-schedule-version re-prompt rules.", ".NET 10 + EF Core + SQLite API (WAL mode) serves config by site key with ETag and logs consent records; rate limited, append-only config.", "A shared JSON Schema (config.schema.json) is the contract between builder, API and embed, with an explicit schemaVersion and a fail-closed rule for newer schemas.", "Optional usage.js is itself gated as a statistics tracker and never sends typed text or personal-looking control names."], "aiAngle": ["Little LLM content: this is a deterministic compliance product, on purpose, because consent logic has to be predictable.", "Built with Claude Code agents, phase by phase, each phase verified in a real browser (puppeteer-core) before the next.", "usage.js feeds AutoDevDocs (autodocumentor), which crawls apps like a user and ranks UI tests by real control usage.", "Fail-closed design (unknown schema, unparseable consent, truthy-but-not-true fields all resolve to no consent) is the same guardrail thinking used for AI systems."], "stack": ["TypeScript", "esbuild", "React", "Vite", "Tailwind", "dnd-kit", ".NET 10", "EF Core", "SQLite", "Netlify", "Cloudflare Tunnel", "JSON Schema"], "metrics": [{"label": "Commits", "value": "6"}, {"label": "Source lines (TS/C#/HTML/CSS)", "value": "3,600+"}, {"label": "Consent checks passing", "value": "25"}, {"label": "Embed bundle", "value": "~16 kb"}, {"label": "Packages", "value": "4"}], "status": "Live MVP, phase 2 on the roadmap", "timeline": "Jul 2026 - Nov 2026", "progress": ["All 7 MVP build phases done: blocker, consent store, loader, banner renderer, API, builder, shared schema.", "Live e2e demo site with an in-page inspector showing blocked trackers and beacons.", "Added usage.js and an admin usage summary (share of sessions per control, two-click paths).", "Mycelium security PR upgraded 7 vulnerable packages.", "Next: cookie auto-scanner, Google Consent Mode v2, multi-tenant auth, consent analytics."], "dogfooding": ["Powers its own demo customer site (Kestrel) with the real embed, live config fetch and audit logging.", "usage.js is consumed by Jason's autodocumentor to prioritise UI tests.", "Dependencies kept patched through his Mycelium scanner PRs."], "links": [{"label": "Live builder", "url": "https://cookieagent.netlify.app/"}, {"label": "Live demo", "url": "https://cookieagent.netlify.app/demo/"}], "buildLogTitle": "Consent gate and git log, Nov 2026", "buildLog": "$ cd embed && npm run test:consent\nPASS  empty -> null\nPASS  unknown format version -> null (fail-closed)\nPASS  missing schedule -> null\nPASS  unknown/truthy-but-not-true field -> coerced false\nPASS  explicit true field -> preserved\nPASS  necessary always forced true\nPASS  save({statistics}) -> statistics granted\nPASS  save partial -> omitted marketing stays false\nPASS  localStorage empty -> falls back to base64 cookie mirror\nPASS  cookie-restored decision applied\n-----------------------------------\nCONSENT GATE PASSED\n\n$ git log --format='%ad %s' --date=short\n2026-11-02 mycelium: upgrade 7 vulnerable package(s)\n2026-10-26 usage.js: which controls visitors click, gated as a statistics tracker\n2026-07-08 switch to stable Tailscale Funnel URL (no domain needed)\n2026-07-07 add live e2e demo site + build-time API base\n2026-07-07 self-hosted consent management platform (MVP)", "slug": "cookieagent", "n": 20, "platforms": "Web", "category": "B2B", "shots": [{"src": "img/cookieagent-00.jpg", "caption": "The banner builder: layout, colours, copy and categories, with a live preview and a one-line embed", "w": 1400, "h": 674}, {"src": "img/cookieagent-01.jpg", "caption": "Kestrel — ship faster (cookietracker e2e demo)", "w": 1400, "h": 875}]}, {"name": "Booktime", "tagline": "Calendar booking app and configurable booking engine", "oneLiner": "A booking page that reads Google Calendar free/busy and creates the event with the guest invited, plus an engine other sites embed for rentals, time slots and group scheduling. The name is being rebranded.", "problem": "Scheduling tools handle one-to-one meetings well but not nights-based rentals, group hangouts like school pickups, or custom workflows. Teams end up with double bookings or stitched-together tools.", "whatItIs": "Booktime is two things. The app gives each user a public booking page tied to their Google account, with in-person, Meet and Zoom options and Hangouts for finding a time with friends. The engine lets another app run bookings under its own sign-in and brand through a partner API, with single or multi-day stays, time slots and multi-person bookings. A Hugo marketing site carries the blog and help docs.", "howItWorks": ["Slot math is pure and tested (lib/slots.js): config, busy blocks and now go in, available times in the visitor's timezone come out.", "Google Calendar calls use plain fetch with no SDK. Booktime creates calendars of its own (calendar.app.created scope) and only reads free/busy from the main calendar, so it never writes to a user's main calendar.", "Stays mode treats a rental night as an interval, so overlap checks, atomic holds, limits and the Google invite work unchanged.", "Hangouts collect free times from friends (connected calendar or tapped slots), rank the best times and book with everyone invited.", "Partner API (/api/partner/*) lets another app run on booktime with its own brand; the key only reaches partner-made accounts, kept out of the by-email index.", "Netlify Functions plus Netlify Blobs for storage, calendar tokens encrypted at rest, sealed cookies, flood guard, Stripe tiers (Free, Pro, Business) and three themes."], "aiAngle": ["Mostly a conventional product: no LLM features in the booking engine, and keeping slot math deterministic is the point.", "Built with Claude Code agents, shipped as small PRs (10 merged PRs on the app).", "Help docs on the marketing site were generated with AutoDevDocs (autodocumentor), which crawls the running app in demo mode like a user and writes the help pages and screenshots.", "The test suite is adversarial (fuzz, forgery, protection, partner isolation), the same mindset used to test AI systems."], "stack": ["Node.js 20", "JavaScript (ESM)", "Netlify Functions", "Netlify Blobs", "Google Calendar API", "Stripe", "SCSS", "Hugo", "Tailwind"], "metrics": [{"label": "Commits (app)", "value": "40"}, {"label": "Tests passing", "value": "122"}, {"label": "App source lines", "value": "7,300+"}, {"label": "Engine modules", "value": "17"}, {"label": "Blog posts", "value": "16"}, {"label": "Help docs pages", "value": "15"}], "status": "Live, pre-launch, rebrand planned", "timeline": "Oct 2026 - Nov 2026", "progress": ["Public booking page on Google Calendar with Meet invites (Oct 27).", "Admin portal, Free/Pro/Business plans, themes (Oct 28).", "Hangouts with maps, video call options and postpone/cancel.", "Stays mode, client SDK and Acme example sites hosted on the engine (Oct 29-30).", "Multiple pages per account, own-calendars-only safety rule, partner mode (Nov 2-5).", "Next: rebrand and public launch."], "dogfooding": ["Originally built as 'Book time with Jason' to book his own client calls.", "His clientHunter project links to it: each outbound email's link carries the lead name, and clientHunter can run on booktime as a partner.", "Acme example sites run live on the engine at /acme."], "links": [{"label": "Sales site", "url": "https://booktime-sales.netlify.app/"}, {"label": "Engine example (Acme)", "url": "https://booktime-app.netlify.app/acme/"}], "buildLogTitle": "Test run and recent commits, Nov 2026", "buildLog": "$ npm test\nok 1 - sealed cookies cannot be forged or replayed after expiry\nok 2 - calendar tokens are encrypted at rest\nok 3 - admin endpoints need a session for an existing account\n...\nok 122 - meeting pages are unchanged\n# tests 122\n# suites 0\n# pass 122\n# fail 0\n# duration_ms 13004\n\n$ git log --format='%ad %s' --date=short\n2026-11-05 Partners: another app can run on booktime with its own sign-in and brand\n2026-11-05 Sign in makes the account; the Pro trial starts at upgrade\n2026-11-02 Cancel only on calendars booktime made and still knows\n2026-11-02 Pages: more than one booking page per account\n2026-11-02 Calendars of booktime's own; never write to the main calendar\n2026-10-30 Acme demo hosted: its own engine at /acme/api\n2026-10-29 Stays mode, a client SDK, and the Acme example sites", "slug": "booktime", "n": 21, "platforms": "Web", "category": "B2B", "shots": [{"src": "img/booktime-00.jpg", "caption": "Marketing site with booking page preview", "w": 1400, "h": 875}, {"src": "img/booktime-01.jpg", "caption": "Acme examples: stays, interview slots and a school-pickup rota on one engine", "w": 1400, "h": 875}, {"src": "img/booktime-02.jpg", "caption": "Working hours: Monday to Friday switched on, each from 09:00 AM to 05:00 PM, and Saturday and Sunday switched off and marked Unavailable", "w": 910, "h": 790}, {"src": "img/booktime-03.jpg", "caption": "The Meetings card with 15 min and 30 min selected out of 15, 20, 30, 45 and 60 min, Selected by default 15 minutes, and Start times every 15 minutes. Below it, Breathing room: Buffer around meetings 10 minutes, Minimum notice 4 hours, Booking window 21 days ahead", "w": 910, "h": 555}, {"src": "img/booktime-04.jpg", "caption": "The admin Overview: Hi Demo!, three tiles reading 3 Upcoming meetings, 4 Booked in the last 7 days and 0 Spam attempts blocked this week, then Your booking link with a Copy button", "w": 1180, "h": 515}, {"src": "img/booktime-05.jpg", "caption": "Upcoming meetings: three bookings, each with a date badge, the person's name and company, the time, length and email, their note, and Join and Event buttons. Recent past meetings (1) is collapsed below", "w": 910, "h": 480}, {"src": "img/booktime-06.jpg", "caption": "The Protection tab: an Always on list of five limits, and a Blocked attempts card reading Nothing blocked, All quiet", "w": 910, "h": 620}, {"src": "img/booktime-07.jpg", "caption": "The Prefilled link card with Name Ada Lovelace, Email ada@example.com, Company Analytical Engines and Meeting length 30 minutes, and below it the finished link starting ?name=Ada+Lovelace&email=ada%40example.com with a Copy button", "w": 910, "h": 405}, {"src": "img/booktime-08.jpg", "caption": "The Your page tab: Your name Jason Sutter, Timezone America/Los Angeles, Page title Book time with Jason, an Intro, and the switch Add a Google Meet link to every booking turned on", "w": 910, "h": 560}, {"src": "img/booktime-09.jpg", "caption": "booktime help", "w": 1400, "h": 875}, {"src": "img/booktime-10.jpg", "caption": "Get early access", "w": 1400, "h": 875}, {"src": "img/booktime-11.jpg", "caption": "Pricing", "w": 1400, "h": 875}, {"src": "img/booktime-12.jpg", "caption": "Availability", "w": 1400, "h": 875}, {"src": "img/booktime-13.jpg", "caption": "Your overview", "w": 1400, "h": 875}]}, {"name": "Glyphwork", "tagline": "SVG icon library and animation studio", "oneLiner": "An icon system plus design studio where you build, edit and animate SVG icons and copy them into a site, for developers and designers.", "problem": "Icon libraries ship one style and are hard to customise or animate. Teams want one consistent set in several styles, with motion, without hand-editing paths.", "whatItIs": "Glyphwork is 746 original icons drawn once on a 24x24 grid and rendered in 8 variants (outline, solid, duotone, sharp, thin, pixel, doodle, sticker). It ships a gw-icon web component, SVG files, sprites, a browser Studio for drawing and keyframe animation, and a Hugo sales and help site. Paid tiers use Lemon Squeezy checkout and Gatekeep sign-in.", "howItWorks": ["icons.json is the single source of truth: ordered groups, one icon per line, shapes with roles (body, line, cutout, dot) so one drawing yields every variant.", "A Rust CLI (builder/) reads icons.json and writes SVGs, sprites, the JS bundle, picker and Studio; contact sheets and pixel bitmaps render with resvg.", "A browser renderer (src/render.js) must match the Rust renderer byte for byte; scripts/parity.mjs compares 5,257 renders with 0 mismatches.", "Motion timeline: per-shape keyframes (move, rotate, scale, opacity, draw-on, point morphs) exported as self-contained SMIL animated SVG that plays even inside img tags.", "gw-icon web component works in plain HTML, Angular, React 19 and Svelte, and under strict CSP via constructed stylesheets.", "Free and Pro tiers: the free build has 84 icons in two styles; sign-in uses Gatekeep passkeys and plans come from the Lemon Squeezy webhook."], "aiAngle": ["No LLM features in the product; it is a deterministic design tool.", "Built with Claude Code agents, including the Rust builder and the parity check that keeps two renderers identical.", "Help center screenshots and pages were generated with AutoDevDocs (autodocumentor) by crawling the Studio.", "Image-to-icon tracing in the Studio is a Pro+ feature (algorithmic, not a model)."], "stack": ["Rust", "resvg", "JavaScript", "Web Components", "Hugo", "SCSS", "Netlify", "Lemon Squeezy", "Gatekeep"], "metrics": [{"label": "Icons", "value": "746"}, {"label": "Styles per icon", "value": "8"}, {"label": "Renders verified identical", "value": "5,257"}, {"label": "Commits", "value": "52"}, {"label": "Source lines (Rust + JS)", "value": "4,300+"}, {"label": "Help pages", "value": "13"}], "status": "Live, paid plans in test mode", "timeline": "Oct 2026 - Nov 2026", "progress": ["126 icons x 8 variants, web component and Studio (Oct 27).", "Rust builder and keyframe timeline; sales site and help center (Oct 27-28).", "Grew to 746 icons in 21 sets (Oct 29).", "Combine icons with badges, stacks and cut layers; free and Pro tiers; Lemon Squeezy checkout.", "Gatekeep passkey accounts and customer dashboard (Oct 29).", "Studio tools: pivots, line weights, Pixel tool, multi-select, point morphs."], "dogfooding": ["Uses Gatekeep, Jason's own auth product, for customer sign-in.", "Counts visits with Zero Trust Analytics, another of his products.", "Help center generated by AutoDevDocs and built on the same Hugo setup as his other products."], "links": [{"label": "Live site and Studio", "url": "https://getglyphwork.com/"}, {"label": "Icon gallery", "url": "https://getglyphwork.com/icons/"}], "buildLogTitle": "Builder checks, Nov 2026", "buildLog": "$ builder/target/release/glyphwork check\nicons.json ok: 746 icons in 21 groups\n\n$ node scripts/parity.mjs\n5257 renders compared, 0 mismatches\n\n$ git log --format='%ad %s' --date=short\n2026-11-03 deps(builder): resvg 0.45 -> 0.49, dropping unmaintained font crates\n2026-11-02 mycelium: upgrade 14 vulnerable package(s)\n2026-11-02 Sitemap: give every page a priority so none drop out\n2026-10-31 Studio: pivot crosshair, line weights, Pixel tool, combining icons\n2026-10-31 Add Zero Trust Analytics visit counting\n2026-10-29 Real accounts: Gatekeep sign-in (passkeys) and plans from the webhook\n2026-10-29 Checkout online (Lemon Squeezy test mode)\n2026-10-29 746 icons: nine more sets matched to common library categories\n2026-10-28 Point morphs: animate individual vertices\n2026-10-27 Rust builder, keyframe timeline, Studio fixes, Netlify config", "slug": "glyphwork", "n": 22, "platforms": "Web", "category": "B2B", "shots": [{"src": "img/glyphwork-00.jpg", "caption": "Sales site", "w": 1400, "h": 875}, {"src": "img/glyphwork-01.jpg", "caption": "Gallery of 746 icons in eight selectable styles", "w": 1400, "h": 875}, {"src": "img/glyphwork-02.jpg", "caption": "The rocket on the board with its body selected: a dashed outline and round handles on each point", "w": 1400, "h": 814}, {"src": "img/glyphwork-03.jpg", "caption": "The fire icon's flame with its tip pulled sideways in the key at 100%, point handles on the board, and the hint bar reading Moving points at 100%", "w": 1400, "h": 1380}, {"src": "img/glyphwork-04.jpg", "caption": "The timeline under the board: motion settings, a row per shape with diamond keyframes, and the red playhead", "w": 1400, "h": 519}, {"src": "img/glyphwork-05.jpg", "caption": "A heart with a brand-new motion called play, a one-second timeline and one row for its shape", "w": 1400, "h": 875}, {"src": "img/glyphwork-06.jpg", "caption": "The Studio with the icon library on the left, the rocket on the drawing board in the middle, the motion timeline below it, and the inspector on the right", "w": 1400, "h": 875}, {"src": "img/glyphwork-07.jpg", "caption": "The Image → icon window with a drop area, the threshold preview and traced result side by side, and Threshold, Smoothing and Ignore specks sliders", "w": 1400, "h": 1202}, {"src": "img/glyphwork-08.jpg", "caption": "Glyphwork help", "w": 1400, "h": 875}, {"src": "img/glyphwork-09.jpg", "caption": "Pricing", "w": 1400, "h": 875}, {"src": "img/glyphwork-10.jpg", "caption": "Draw on the grid", "w": 1400, "h": 875}, {"src": "img/glyphwork-11.jpg", "caption": "Edit shapes and points", "w": 1400, "h": 875}, {"src": "img/glyphwork-12.jpg", "caption": "Export icons", "w": 1400, "h": 875}]}];
